// Which orders reach her, in what order, and the sentence that says why.
//
// Mirrors src/lib/awards/relevance.ts on purpose — same shape, same discipline:
// a numeric score exists ONLY to sort, and is never rendered. What she reads is
// whyThisOrder(), a cascading template where the first true condition wins.
//
// The rule that keeps this honest, from the plan:
//   If Hunter cannot state the reason in one sentence she would understand,
//   the item does not rank.
//
// Nothing in this file is generated by a model. The highest-stakes copy in the
// product is a string template filled from record facts, because a hallucination
// on "you supplied this in March" is a hallucination she acts on.
import type { MicroOrder } from "./order";
import { MICRO_ORDERS, TODAY } from "../mock/microPurchaseOrders";
import { corpusNow, orderValue, pricePoint, usd, monthYear, hasUsableCeiling } from "./pricing";
import { isMultiplied, unitNoun, isKnownUnit } from "./units";
import { ASK_MENTOR_FIRST } from "./mentor";
import { CATEGORY_LABEL, type MicroCategory } from "./categories";

/** FAR 2.101, raised 1 October 2025. Not $10,000 — that figure is superseded. */
export const MICRO_PURCHASE_LIMIT = 15000;

// ── Stage 1 · permission ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
// AMC and AMSC are a PAIR. The number is HOW DLA is buying the item today; the
// letter is WHY it is restricted.
//
// **THE SOURCE IS DFARS PGI 217.75, EFFECTIVE 05/07/2026.** Fetched 10 Sep 2026
// from `https://www.acquisition.gov/dfarspgi/pgi-217.75-acquisition-replenishment-parts`
// — section 2-201.1 is the AMC list, section 2-201.2 is the AMSC list, and
// Exhibit I is the table of valid combinations. Every value below cites it.
//
// It replaces `govbeacon.io`, `samsearch.co/blog` and `static.ilsmart.com`.
// Those three commercial pages carried the one verdict on this surface that
// decides whether she may bid, while every other claim here is FAR-cited or
// DoD-cited. This comment used to open "The AMC value table is UNSOURCED ...
// The permission verdict ships on it." That is no longer true, and **§15 B9 is
// closed by the fetch above**.
//
// THE LETTER RULES, AND THE NUMBER STILL NEVER REFUSES (decided 20 Aug 2026).
// `restrictionOf()` used to test the METHOD first, so the number overrode the
// letter and the same AMSC produced opposite verdicts: `1C` read "Only approved
// sellers can quote this one. The form is called a Source Approval Request."
// while `3C` read "Only the maker can supply this one … There is no form that
// changes that." plus "Skip this one." — one engineering-source-approval code,
// two rulings, ~900px apart on `/micro-purchases/restricted`. `3Z` asserted
// sole manufacture over the letter `isCommercial()` in this same file calls a
// commercial item.
//
// **THE REASON FOR THAT DECISION HAS CHANGED, AND THE DECISION HAS NOT.** It
// used to rest on the AMC table being unsourced, and an unsourced axis may not
// produce a permanent refusal. The document now gives a better reason. 2-202(d):
// "One-time acquisition of a part by a method other than indicated by the code
// does not require a change to the AMC (e.g., when only one of a number of
// sources can meet a short delivery date ...)". 2-203(a): "A code assigned to a
// part should never be considered fixed with respect to either technical
// circumstance or time". So an AMC of 3, 4 or 5 says how DLA is buying the part
// TODAY. It is neither a bar on her nor a permanent one.
//
// **THE METHOD DOES DESCRIBE THIS ORDER, AND SINCE 10 SEPTEMBER 2026 IT SAYS
// SO.** Withholding the affirmative was the whole of what the number did, and
// an order whose letter was open under AMC 3, 4 or 5 went to a mentor — so the
// board said "I am not sure who may sell this one" over 56 of the 60 orders in
// that group on a live 500-row page, every one of them a record we could read
// in full. `closedByMethod()` is the tier that says what the number says: DLA
// has one supplier for THIS order. It never produces an affirmative, and
// `mayBid()` is unchanged.
//
// **WHAT THE PAIR DOES REFUSE IS AN IMPOSSIBLE COMBINATION.** Exhibit I lists
// which AMC each AMSC may appear beside, and `1D` and `2D` are not among them.
// That is a DATA ERROR — a claim about the record, not about her permission —
// and it gets its own branch and its own sentence. See `VALID_METHODS`.

/**
 * The acquisition method codes that say DLA is competing this part.
 *
 * PGI 217.75 2-201.1, in full: **1** "Suitable for competitive acquisition for
 * the second or subsequent time" · **2** "Suitable for competitive acquisition
 * for the first time" · **3** "Acquire, for the second or subsequent time,
 * directly from the actual manufacturer" · **4** "Acquire, for the first time,
 * directly from the actual manufacturer" · **5** "Acquire directly from a sole
 * source contractor which is not the actual manufacturer" · **0** the part
 * "was not assigned AMC 1 through 5 when it entered the inventory, nor has it
 * ever completed screening", which is why `codesKnown()` reads it as a
 * sentinel rather than as a value.
 *
 * Strings, because that is what the API returns. Live values seen: "1","2","3","0".
 * This was a numeric union, which could not hold "0" and coerced it to falsy.
 */
const OPEN_METHODS = ["1", "2"];

/**
 * The letters that let an order onto the board.
 *
 * **G is the open letter, and the document says it is the only one.** PGI
 * 217.75 2-201.2(g): "The Government has rights to the technical data, the data
 * package is complete, and there are no technical data, engineering, tooling or
 * manufacturing restrictions. (This is the only AMSC that implies that parts
 * are candidates for full and open competition. Other AMSCs such as K, M, N, Q,
 * and S may imply limited competition when two or more independent sources
 * exist yet the technical data package is inadequate for full and open
 * competition.)"
 *
 * **Z is the commercial letter, and this file no longer calls it full and
 * open.** 2-201.2(z): "This part is a commercial product, nondevelopmental
 * item, or commercially available off-the-shelf item." The paragraph above
 * excludes it from the full-and-open finding, and nothing here claims one for
 * it: what makes a `1Z` or a `2Z` biddable is the AMC beside it, which says DLA
 * is competing the buy. `mayBid()` requires both halves, so the PAIR carries
 * the verdict and neither code carries it alone.
 *
 * L and U were in this set and are now out — 2-201.2(l) is the below-threshold
 * screening code and 2-201.2(u) is "The cost to the Government to breakout this
 * part and acquire it competitively has been determined to exceed the projected
 * savings over the life span of the part". Both are the Government declining to
 * compete the item, not an invitation. That call was taken on 19 August 2026
 * from secondary references; the primary source confirms it.
 */
const OPEN_SUFFIXES = ["G", "Z"];

/**
 * Restricted, but with a documented route: a Source Approval Request.
 *
 * **C AND ONLY C.** PGI 217.75 2-201.2(c): the part "requires engineering
 * source approval by the design control activity in order to maintain the
 * quality of the part ... An alternate source must qualify in accordance with
 * the design control activity's procedures, as approved by the cognizant
 * Government engineering activity." That qualification procedure IS the route.
 *
 * **B LEFT THIS SET ON 10 SEPTEMBER 2026, AND THE PRIMARY SOURCE IS WHY.** All
 * three commercial references grouped B with C as "requires a Source Approval
 * Request", and 2-201.2(b) says the opposite: "Suitable technical data,
 * Government data rights, or manufacturing knowledge are not available to
 * permit acquisition from other sources, nor qualification testing of another
 * part, nor use of a second source part in the intended application." It denies
 * both mechanisms a route would use and names no procedure, while the very next
 * paragraph names one for C. Sending her to file a request the regulation says
 * cannot succeed is the permissive error, and this file's own header calls that
 * worse than saying nothing. B now reads `NO_ROUTE_REASONS`.
 *
 * **D NEVER BELONGED HERE EITHER**, and that is settled below on the same
 * document rather than left open.
 */
const SAR_FIXABLE_SUFFIXES = ["C"];

/**
 * Restricted with no route, and ONE REASON PER LETTER.
 *
 * A `Record`, not a set, because the sentence on screen must be true of the
 * letter it is printed over. This was an array of letters sharing one sentence,
 * and that is how "The government does not own the drawings." came to be given
 * for five letters when it is a data-rights fact about two — the S, V and Y
 * correction of 20 August 2026. Adding a letter here costs a sentence now,
 * which is the price of not making that mistake again.
 *
 * Each reading is PGI 217.75 2-201.2's own:
 *
 *  · **B** (b) — a source control or selected item drawing names the
 *    manufacturers, and the Government holds neither the data, nor the rights,
 *    nor the manufacturing knowledge to add another source or to qualify a
 *    substitute part. See `SAR_FIXABLE_SUFFIXES` for why B is here and not
 *    there.
 *
 *  · **D** (d) — "The data needed to acquire this part competitively is not
 *    physically available, it cannot be obtained economically, nor is it
 *    possible to draft adequate specifications or any other adequate,
 *    economical description of the material for a competitive solicitation."
 *    **THIS SETTLES D**, which two secondary references read two ways — one as
 *    a restricted source WITH an approval route, the other as the Government
 *    being unable to compete the item at all. The document says the second, so
 *    D is not a route and never belonged in the Source Approval Request tier.
 *    Exhibit I confirms it from the other side: D pairs only with AMC 3, 4 or
 *    5, the three methods that buy from one named company, and never with 1 or
 *    D is the most common letter this surface sees — 8 of the 30 ruled orders
 *    in `liveMapperOutput.fixture.ts`, and 32.2% of a 267-record sample the
 *    older comments in this file quote from a research file this repository no
 *    longer holds, which is why that share is named here as a record and not
 *    re-used as a figure. Either way it spent three weeks telling a large part
 *    of the board "I am not sure who may sell this one." It is ruled now.
 *
 *  · **P** (p) and **R** (r) — the Government does not own the rights to the
 *    data, cannot economically obtain them, and reverse engineering the part is
 *    uneconomical. This branch used to end "We have not checked this." We have
 *    now checked it, so the hedge is gone and the sentence states the fact.
 */
const RIGHTS_NOT_OWNED =
  "The government does not own the rights to the drawings for this part. It cannot buy those rights at a fair price. So it cannot add new sellers.";

const NO_ROUTE_REASONS: Record<string, string> = {
  B: "The drawing for this part names the companies that may make it. The government does not have the information to add another company. It cannot accept a different part in its place either.",
  D: "The government cannot get the information another company would need to make this part. It cannot write a good enough description of the part either. So it cannot add new sellers.",
  // One string, two letters, on purpose: P and R are the same fact about the
  // same thing, and writing it twice is how a shared sentence drifts.
  P: RIGHTS_NOT_OWNED,
  R: RIGHTS_NOT_OWNED,
};

/**
 * The letters whose own paragraph makes an AMC of 3, 4 or 5 mean ONE supplier.
 *
 * **THIS IS THE METHOD SPEAKING, NOT THE LETTER.** Each paragraph below carries
 * the same conditional: the higher acquisition method codes are valid only in
 * the case where a single source can supply the part. So a record pairing one
 * of these letters with AMC 3, 4 or 5 states, on the document's own terms, that
 * DLA has one supplier for this part today. `closedByMethod()` turns that into
 * a sentence, and until this feature every one of them said "I am not sure who
 * may sell this one." over a record that names the answer.
 *
 * **ONE SENTENCE OVER ELEVEN LETTERS IS SAFE HERE, and the S/V/Y correction is
 * why that needs saying.** That mistake gave five letters a data-rights fact
 * true of two. The sentence this set earns asserts ONLY the clause every
 * paragraph below carries, and asserts nothing about WHY the letter was
 * assigned. Each letter's own wording, from PGI 217.75 2-201.2:
 *
 *  · **A** (a), **H** (h), **L** (l), **Q** (q), **U** (u), **V** (v) and
 *    **Y** (y) — "If one source is available, AMCs 3, 4, or 5 are valid."
 *  · **K** (k) — "If one source has such castings and cannot provide them to
 *    other sources, AMCs 3, 4, or 5 are valid."
 *  · **M** (m) — "If only one set of tooling exists and cannot be made
 *    available to another source for manufacture of this part, AMCs 3, 4, or 5
 *    are valid."
 *  · **N** (n) — "If the test cannot be made available for the competitive
 *    manufacture of the part ... valid AMCs are 3, 4, or 5."
 *  · **S** (s) — "If one source is approved, AMCs 3, 4, or 5 are valid."
 *
 * B, C, D, P, R and Z carry the same clause and are ABSENT, because a ruling on
 * the letter itself already answers them: B, D, P and R read `NO_ROUTE_REASONS`,
 * C reads `SAR_FIXABLE_SUFFIXES`, and Z is handled by `closedByMethod()`'s
 * first branch with a sentence of its own. G, T and O are absent because their
 * paragraphs authorise no such pair at all — G and T are "AMCs 1 or 2 are
 * valid", O is "Only AMC O is valid" — so `pairIsImpossible()` reaches those
 * records first and calls them a data error.
 *
 * **UNDER AMC 1 OR 2 TEN OF THESE LETTERS KEEP ABSTAINING, and that is a
 * decision rather than a gap.** Each paragraph's other branch says at least two
 * sources exist and DLA is competing the buy. That is not an invitation:
 * 2-201.2(g) calls G "the only AMSC that implies that parts are candidates for
 * full and open competition" and names K, M, N, Q and S as codes that "may
 * imply limited competition when two or more independent sources exist yet the
 * technical data package is inadequate for full and open competition".
 *
 * **Q IS THE ONE THAT NO LONGER ABSTAINS.** Limited competition is a tier this
 * surface now has, and `APPROVED_SOURCE_REASONS` holds it with Q's own
 * sentence. The other four letters (g) names — K, M, N and S — are not in that
 * record: each needs its paragraph read and written into a sentence of its own,
 * and no pair on the live board carries one of them beside AMC 1 or 2. A, H, L,
 * U, V and Y are not named by (g) at all and keep reaching a mentor.
 */
const SINGLE_SUPPLIER_SUFFIXES = ["A", "H", "K", "L", "M", "N", "Q", "S", "U", "V", "Y"];

/**
 * The letters whose paragraph makes an AMC of 1 or 2 mean LIMITED competition.
 *
 * **THE TIER `SINGLE_SUPPLIER_SUFFIXES` ABSTAINED FOR, AND ONE LETTER ENTERS
 * IT.** That set's last paragraph says why the eleven keep abstaining under AMC
 * 1 and 2: 2-201.2(g) calls G "the only AMSC that implies that parts are
 * candidates for full and open competition" and names "K, M, N, Q, and S" as
 * codes that "may imply limited competition when two or more independent
 * sources exist yet the technical data package is inadequate for full and open
 * competition". Limited competition is a verdict. It is not an open one, and it
 * is not a refusal either: DLA competes the buy among the sellers it already
 * has.
 *
 * **A `Record`, NOT A SET, AND ONE REASON PER LETTER.** The rule
 * `NO_ROUTE_REASONS` carries, for the reason it carries it: the sentence on
 * screen must be true of the letter it is printed over, and the five letters (g)
 * names do not share a paragraph. The S, V and Y correction is what one shared
 * sentence costs.
 *
 * **Q ALONE TODAY.** 2-201.2(q): "The Government does not have adequate data,
 * lacks rights to data, or both needed to purchase this part from additional
 * sources. The Government has been unable to economically buy the data or
 * rights to the data ... Breakout to competition has not been achieved ... If
 * at least two sources exist, AMCs 1 or 2 are valid." Two or more sources, DLA
 * competing among them, and no procedure named by which a sixth seller joins
 * them. That is this tier, and the sentence below states exactly those facts.
 * **K, M AND N AND S ARE NOT REFUSED HERE — THEY ARE UNREAD.** Each carries its
 * own paragraph, none of those paragraphs is transcribed into a sentence here,
 * and no pair on the live board carries one of them beside AMC 1 or 2. They
 * keep abstaining until a reader takes each paragraph on its own.
 *
 * **IT IS NOT THE SOURCE APPROVAL REQUEST TIER AND MUST NEVER READ LIKE ONE.**
 * 2-201.2(c) names the procedure an alternate source qualifies under, and that
 * procedure is what earns `SAR_FIXABLE_SUFFIXES` its route and its "file a
 * Source Approval Request" step. (q) names no procedure at all. B and D were
 * removed from that tier for exactly this reason — see both docblocks — so a
 * letter joins it on a paragraph that names the procedure, never on "it is also
 * restricted".
 *
 * **T IS IN NEITHER TIER, AND IT HAS ITS OWN NOW.** See `QPL_SUFFIXES` below.
 * 2-201.2(t) is limited competition WITH a procedure, and the procedure is a
 * qualified products list rather than a Source Approval Request. This tier
 * would tell her no way in exists; the SAR tier would send her to the wrong
 * form. Neither sentence is true of T, so T is ruled beside these two rather
 * than inside either.
 *
 * Every paragraph quoted above was read on 16 September 2026 from
 * `backend/src/small-orders/small-orders.acquisition-codes.ts`, which
 * transcribes DFARS PGI 217.75 (effective 05/07/2026) verbatim and carries the
 * page it was taken from and the day a person read it.
 */
const APPROVED_SOURCE_REASONS: Record<string, string> = {
  Q: "DLA is asking more than one seller for a price on this part. The government does not have the data or the rights another seller would need. So it cannot add a seller to this order. There is no form for this one.",
};

/**
 * Restricted to a qualified products list, which is a route of its own.
 *
 * **T AND ONLY T.** PGI 217.75 2-201.2(t), the whole paragraph: "Acquisition of
 * this part is controlled by qualified products list (QPL) procedures.
 * Competition for this part is limited to sources which are listed on or are
 * qualified for listing on the QPL at the time of award (see FAR Part 9 and
 * DFARS Part 209). AMCs 1 or 2 are valid."
 *
 * **IT IS A THIRD TIER BECAUSE THE OTHER TWO WOULD BOTH LIE.** `SAR_FIXABLE_SUFFIXES`
 * ends in a Source Approval Request, and (t) names a different procedure —
 * sending her to file that request is the permissive error this file records
 * for B and D. `APPROVED_SOURCE_REASONS` ends in "There is no form for this
 * one", and listing on a QPL is a real way in, so that sentence is false here.
 * A tier is earned by the paragraph naming its own route, and (t) names one.
 *
 * **AMC 1 AND 2 ONLY, AND THE PARAGRAPH SAYS SO ITSELF.** Exhibit I gives T the
 * row `12` and `VALID_METHODS` carries it, so `pairIsImpossible()` reaches a
 * `3T`, `4T` or `5T` record first and calls it a data error. That was true
 * before this tier existed and nothing here changes it. `qualifiedListOnly()`
 * still tests `methodIsOpen()` for the reason `approvedSourcesOnly()` does: the
 * claim is about a COMPETITIVE buy, and 2-201.1(b) and (c) are the only two
 * paragraphs that describe one.
 *
 * **AN ARRAY, NOT A `Record`, AND THAT IS NOT THE B/D SHORTCUT.** The two
 * `Record` tables above exist because a sentence has to be true of the letter
 * it is printed over, and those tiers hold letters whose paragraphs differ. One
 * letter, one paragraph, one sentence — the same shape `SAR_FIXABLE_SUFFIXES`
 * has. A second letter joins this set only on a paragraph that names a
 * qualified products list, and it would arrive with its own sentence.
 *
 * The paragraph above was read on 16 September 2026 from
 * `backend/src/small-orders/small-orders.acquisition-codes.ts`, which
 * transcribes DFARS PGI 217.75 (effective 05/07/2026) verbatim and carries the
 * page it was taken from and the day a person read it.
 */
const QPL_SUFFIXES = ["T"];

/**
 * Which acquisition method codes each suffix may lawfully appear beside.
 *
 * **Exhibit I to PGI 217.75, transcribed.** The value is the AMC digits the
 * exhibit marks valid (`o`); every other digit it marks invalid (`X`). The
 * prose in 2-201.2 says the same thing letter by letter, and the two agree on
 * every row — most letters read "If one source is available, AMCs 3, 4, or 5
 * are valid. If at least two sources exist, AMCs 1 or 2 are valid", which is
 * all five.
 *
 * A letter absent from this table pairs with NO method. 2-201.2 marks E, F, J
 * and W "(Reserved)" and I and X "(Not authorized)", and Exhibit I gives none
 * of the six a row.
 *
 * AMC `0` appears here only against AMSC `O`, which is its matching "never
 * screened" sentinel. `codesKnown()` already refuses a `0` on either half, so
 * that pair is read as the admitted gap and never reaches the pair check.
 *
 * 2-202(c) is why this is a DATA ERROR and not a verdict: "Restricted
 * combinations of AMC/AMSCs are reflected in the AMSC definitions. The Defense
 * Logistics Information Service will reject invalid code combinations, as shown
 * in Exhibit I, submitted for entry into the Federal catalog program."
 */
const VALID_METHODS: Record<string, string> = {
  A: "12345",
  B: "12345",
  C: "12345",
  D: "345",
  G: "12",
  H: "12345",
  K: "12345",
  L: "12345",
  M: "12345",
  N: "12345",
  O: "0",
  P: "12345",
  Q: "12345",
  R: "12345",
  S: "12345",
  T: "12",
  U: "12345",
  V: "12345",
  Y: "12345",
  Z: "12345",
};

/**
 * Is this AMC/AMSC pair one DLA's own coding rules reject?
 *
 * TRUE only when BOTH halves are readable and Exhibit I marks the combination
 * invalid. A missing or unreadable half is not a contradiction — it is the
 * admitted gap, and `codesKnown()` owns that sentence. Abstaining here is the
 * whole point: a data error is an accusation about the record, and this surface
 * only makes one when it can see both codes.
 */
function pairIsImpossible(o: MicroOrder): boolean {
  const method = o.amc?.trim();
  const suffix = o.amsc?.trim().toUpperCase();
  if (method == null || suffix == null) return false;
  if (!/^[0-5]$/.test(method) || !/^[A-Z]$/.test(suffix)) return false;
  // `Object.hasOwn`, not a bare lookup: `amsc` arrives from the DIBBS feed, and
  // the same rule `units.ts` follows applies to every table keyed by it.
  const valid = Object.hasOwn(VALID_METHODS, suffix) ? VALID_METHODS[suffix] : "";
  return !valid.includes(method);
}

/** Can she get approved to bid this one, given time and a form? */
export function isSarFixable(o: MicroOrder): boolean {
  return o.amsc != null && SAR_FIXABLE_SUFFIXES.includes(o.amsc.trim().toUpperCase());
}

/** A code we read but cannot rule on. Not open, not closed, not a known route.
 *  Module-private on purpose: `restrictionOf()` is the only thing entitled to
 *  turn it into a sentence, and an exported helper nothing renders is `D-D9`. */
function isUnverifiedSuffix(o: MicroOrder): boolean {
  const a = o.amsc?.trim().toUpperCase();
  if (!a || !/^[A-Z]$/.test(a)) return false;
  // ANY readable letter we hold no ruling for.
  //
  // D used to be the largest member of this tier and is now ruled from the
  // primary source, but the argument that put it here was never specific to D:
  // the record says "H" perfectly clearly, so "I could not read who may quote
  // it" is a false statement about DLA's record, and "DLA is not taking new
  // sellers for this one" is a false statement about DLA's intent. We read the
  // letter. We have no ruling for it. Those are different facts and only the
  // second one is ours to admit.
  //
  // What is left here is every letter PGI 217.75 2-201.2 describes and does not
  // resolve into a verdict this surface can render. L is the one worth naming
  // now: (l) is the below-threshold screening code, 2-201.2(g) does not name
  // it, and it is the largest pair still reaching a mentor.
  //
  // **THIS PREDICATE IS NO LONGER THE LAST WORD ON THESE LETTERS.**
  // `restrictionOf()` runs `closedByMethod()` first, and eleven of them —
  // `SINGLE_SUPPLIER_SUFFIXES` — are ruled there when the method is 3, 4 or 5.
  // It then runs `approvedSourcesOnly()`, which rules Q under AMC 1 and 2, and
  // `qualifiedListOnly()`, which rules T there. So a letter this returns `true`
  // for still reaches the mentor only under AMC 1 or 2, and only where neither
  // of those two tiers claims it.
  // T never reaches a mentor now under any method: 2-201.2(t) authorises AMC 1
  // and 2 alone, so `3T` is a data error and `1T` is the qualified-list tier.
  //
  // Before this predicate existed, every letter outside the ruled sets rendered
  // the unreadable-code sentence, appeared on NO list — neither biddable nor
  // restricted — and reached the order page by URL alone, where it offered the
  // full price ladder under a headline saying we could not tell if she may bid.
  // Silent loss (`D-C1`) and a dead end (`D-D3`). The share this tier held was
  // measured at 15.7% of a 267-record sample, and that sample described a set D
  // was still in; the figure is not restated for today's set because the file
  // holding it is not in this repository and the measurement cannot be re-run.
  return (
    !OPEN_SUFFIXES.includes(a) &&
    !SAR_FIXABLE_SUFFIXES.includes(a) &&
    !Object.hasOwn(NO_ROUTE_REASONS, a)
  );
}

/**
 * Can we READ this suffix at all?
 *
 * ANY SINGLE LETTER IS READABLE. That is the whole of what this function
 * answers, and the docblock here used to describe the code before that change —
 * it said the unruled letters reach "I cannot tell if you can bid this one",
 * which is the UNREADABLE-code sentence and a different page (`D-B4`).
 *
 * What actually happens: A, H, K, L, M, N, Q, S, T, U, V and Y are the letters
 * PGI 217.75 2-201.2 describes without resolving on the letter alone. Under AMC
 * 1 or 2 most of them are `restrictionOf()`'s `unknown` branch: "Not clear who
 * may sell this one. / No clear meaning for this DLA code at source. Ask your
 * mentor…" on the board. Q and T are ruled there instead, by
 * `approvedSourcesOnly()` and `qualifiedListOnly()`. Under AMC 3, 4 or 5 eleven
 * of them are ruled by `closedByMethod()`, on the "one source is available"
 * clause each paragraph carries; T is not, because it authorises no such pair.
 * Whether we hold a RULING is that function's question, not this one's.
 *
 * The set-membership chain that used to sit under the letter test was
 * UNREACHABLE. Every member of every one of those sets is a single character,
 * so the test above already answered `true` for all of them, and only a
 * multi-character value could reach the chain — which no set held. Dropping the
 * four `includes` calls changes no answer this function has ever given, and
 * `relevance.acquisitionCodes.test.ts` pins that rather than asserting it here.
 */
function suffixUnderstood(o: MicroOrder): boolean {
  const a = o.amsc?.trim().toUpperCase();
  if (!a) return false;
  // Any single letter is READABLE. Whether we can rule on it is a different
  // question, answered by `restrictionOf()`'s unverified branch. This predicate
  // used to conflate the two, which is how ten letters ended up telling her we
  // could not read a code we had read.
  return /^[A-Z]$/.test(a);
}

/** Unknown is not open, and it is also not closed. Callers must check
 *  `codesKnown` before drawing any conclusion from these two. */
export function codesKnown(o: MicroOrder): boolean {
  // "0" is a SENTINEL, not a value. AMC runs 1-5 and AMSC is a letter; a zero in
  // either means DLA has assigned no code. Treating non-null as known read an
  // unassigned code as sole-source and refused an order she is very probably
  // eligible for — and because the only multiplied unit of issue in the corpus
  // carries "00", it also made the 100x path unreachable on every screen in the
  // product. One sentinel, two criticals.
  const method = o.amc != null && /^[1-5]$/.test(o.amc.trim());
  const suffix = o.amsc != null && /^[A-Za-z]$/.test(o.amsc.trim());
  // A letter we have no ruling for is not a known code. "Limited competition"
  // is not a verdict we can render either way, so it goes to the honest state.
  return method && suffix && suffixUnderstood(o);
}
export function methodIsOpen(o: MicroOrder): boolean {
  return o.amc != null && OPEN_METHODS.includes(o.amc.trim());
}
export function suffixIsOpen(o: MicroOrder): boolean {
  return o.amsc != null && OPEN_SUFFIXES.includes(o.amsc.trim().toUpperCase());
}
export function mayBid(o: MicroOrder): boolean {
  // A PAIR EXHIBIT I REJECTS NEVER PRODUCES AN AFFIRMATIVE. No such pair can
  // satisfy the two tests beside it today — G is the only open letter the
  // exhibit restricts, and it restricts G to exactly `OPEN_METHODS` — so this
  // clause changes no answer over any corpus we hold. It is here because the
  // guarantee has to be structural: "she is never told she may bid on a record
  // that contradicts itself" is the one direction of error this surface cannot
  // afford, and it must not rest on two tables happening to agree.
  return codesKnown(o) && !pairIsImpossible(o) && methodIsOpen(o) && suffixIsOpen(o);
}
/**
 * Is this a commercial item by its acquisition suffix?
 *
 * NO LONGER A FILTER. v1 shipped "commercial items only" as a conservative
 * narrowing, reasoning from the code table rather than from the corpus.
 *
 * MEASUREMENT CORRECTED — the first one was sample bias, and this comment
 * carried the withdrawn version. It read "ZERO of 151. The board would have
 * launched empty." That was measured entirely on one-day-old postings, where a
 * DIBBS record has not finished arriving. Re-measured on mature (eight-day)
 * postings: commercial-only yields roughly **39 orders a day**, not zero, and
 * the research file withdraws "would have launched empty" in terms.
 *
 * The decision still stands, on its own evidence and not on the overstatement:
 * dropping the filter multiplies her inventory about **six-fold** (~39 to ~234
 * a day under the cap), and the 10.9% pass rate is stable across both samples.
 * Kept only to soften the sourcing sentence on an order we can say is openly
 * sold.
 *
 * `[research: micro-purchases CORRECTION 2, and Takeaway 21 — measure a corpus
 * at the age you will read it at]`
 */
export function isCommercial(o: MicroOrder): boolean {
  return o.amsc === "Z";
}

export type Restriction = {
  /** Can a form fix this? A suffix restriction, yes. A method restriction, no.
   *  Kept because the restricted page sorts on it; `route` is the fact. */
  fixable: boolean;
  /** FIVE states, because there are five — and a boolean holding three facts
   *  is how "ask your mentor" ended up printed directly above "skip this one".
   *  `sar`      — a Source Approval Request can genuinely fix this (C).
   *  `approved` — DLA competes the buy among the sellers it already has, and
   *               the paragraph names no procedure by which another joins them
   *               (`APPROVED_SOURCE_REASONS`). It is NOT `sar`: that tier ends
   *               in a form, and this one ends in no form. Two states that
   *               share a next step may share a route; these two do not.
   *  `qpl`      — DLA buys only from sellers on its qualified products list for
   *               the part, or from sellers qualified for listing when it awards
   *               (`QPL_SUFFIXES`). Limited competition again, and a THIRD state
   *               rather than either one above it: (t) names a procedure, so
   *               `approved`'s "there is no form for this one" is false here,
   *               and the procedure is not the one (c) names, so `sar` would
   *               send her to the wrong form. The route is real and it runs
   *               longer than the order, which is what the copy says.
   *  `none`     — nothing fixes it. The honest next step is another order.
   *  `unknown`  — a human looks first. TWO facts share it, because on both the
   *               next step is the same person: a letter PGI 217.75 describes
   *               and we hold no ruling for, and a PAIR Exhibit I rejects. Their
   *               headlines and bodies differ, so the restricted page groups them
   *               apart; only the next step is shared.
   *  A page that ends in a next step must know WHICH next step, and `fixable`
   *  is false for `none`, `unknown`, `approved` and `qpl` while they need
   *  different copy. `fixable` marks the Source Approval Request tier alone:
   *  qualifying for a QPL is a route, and it is not the form that flag names. */
  route: "sar" | "approved" | "qpl" | "none" | "unknown";
  headline: string;
  body: string;
  code: string;
};

/** Said on every surface that refuses an order she cannot win. Defined once:
 *  the same warning sentence lived in two files and diverged within a day. */
export const SKIP_THIS_ONE = "Skip this one. Your time is better spent on the open orders.";

/**
 * What a set-aside actually asks of her.
 *
 * The first version told every set-aside order she needed "a certificate" and
 * offered to skip. Four of five live set-asides are SBA — a TOTAL SMALL
 * BUSINESS set-aside, which requires no certificate at all: she represents
 * herself as small in SAM against that solicitation's size standard. Set-asides
 * are half of live DIBBS, so that copy was telling a qualifying small business
 * to walk away from the easiest orders on the board. A silent loss caused by
 * our own words.
 *
 * Only these need an SBA-issued certification.
 */
/**
 * Set-asides she can enter by declaring her own size, with no certificate.
 * Anything not in this set and not in CERTIFIED_SET_ASIDES is a code we hold no
 * ruling for, and the verdict abstains rather than guessing.
 */
const SELF_CERTIFIED_SET_ASIDES = new Set(["SBA", "SB", "SBP", "TOTAL SMALL BUSINESS"]);

const CERTIFIED_SET_ASIDES: Record<string, string> = {
  WOSB: "women-owned small businesses",
  EDWOSB: "economically disadvantaged women-owned small businesses",
  SDVOSB: "service-disabled veteran-owned small businesses",
  SDVOSBC: "service-disabled veteran-owned small businesses",
  // "HUBZone businesses" restated the acronym as its own expansion — the rule
  // run in a circle. The official term still renders beneath, on the set-aside
  // block, so this slot says what the thing IS.
  HZC: "businesses in an area the government wants to bring jobs to",
  HUBZONE: "businesses in an area the government wants to bring jobs to",
  // Same rule as HUBZone above: the slot says what the thing IS, in her words,
  // and the official designator renders beneath it on the set-aside block. "SBA"
  // was the whole label and is expanded nowhere on this surface — the acronym
  // rule, and the plain-English gate held it against this lane by name.
  "8A": "small companies the government picked for extra help",
  "8AN": "small companies the government picked for extra help",
};

export type SetAsideNote = {
  needsCertificate: boolean;
  /** The group DLA reserved it for — **null when we hold no ruling for the
   *  code**, so no surface can print a group we have not identified. */
  who: string | null;
  headline: string;
  body: string;
};

/**
 * Can she actually send a price on this today?
 *
 * `dibbs_status` is 100% populated live, and "Pending" means DLA has posted the
 * solicitation but is NOT yet taking quotes — it clears to "Open" within days.
 * The field was stored, documented, and read by nothing, so nine of eighteen
 * orders rendered the full send block and the "Go to DLA and quote this"
 * button. She goes, finds no quote box, and concludes she did it wrong — the
 * exact fear this surface exists to design against. Worse, the tap wrote a
 * QuoteRecord saying she had sent a price.
 */
export function acceptsQuotesNow(o: MicroOrder): boolean {
  // Unknown is NOT open — the same rule passesDelivery(), passesSize() and
  // the other predicates already apply to their own nulls. This branch used to
  // return true on a null status, so a record whose status never arrived went
  // on the board as quotable: she goes to DIBBS, finds no quote box, and
  // concludes she did it wrong. dibbsStatus is 100% populated live, so the
  // generosity bought nothing and risked the one harm the docstring names.
  return o.dibbsStatus?.trim().toLowerCase() === "open";
}
/** We hold a record but no status for it. Not the same as "posted, not open". */
export function statusIsUnknown(o: MicroOrder): boolean {
  return o.dibbsStatus == null || o.dibbsStatus.trim() === "";
}

export function setAsideNote(o: MicroOrder): SetAsideNote | null {
  const raw = o.setAside?.trim().toUpperCase();
  if (!raw) return null;
  const who = CERTIFIED_SET_ASIDES[raw];
  if (who) {
    return {
      needsCertificate: true,
      who,
      headline: `DLA is keeping this one for ${who}.`,
      body: "You need that certificate before you quote. Check that you have it.",
    };
  }
  // SELF-CERTIFIED SMALL BUSINESS — and ONLY the values we recognise as that.
  //
  // "Anything else we do not recognise" used to land here, which meant every
  // unrecognised code was told to her as an open small-business set-aside. The
  // sole-source variants — WOSBSS, HZS, WOSBS — read "DLA is keeping this one
  // for small companies." over a full send block, so she would quote a job only
  // one named company may win, spend the afternoon and the postage, and be
  // disqualified. Wrong in the permissive direction is the expensive one.
  //
  // An unrecognised code now abstains, below.
  // "You say you are small when you register" described the one-time SAM
  // representation and stopped there. The operative act is per solicitation:
  // she represents herself as small against THAT order's size standard, with
  // the offer (FAR 52.219-1). A company can be small on one order and large on
  // the next, and the old sentence read as a complete account of a test it did
  // not describe.
  if (SELF_CERTIFIED_SET_ASIDES.has(raw)) {
    return {
      needsCertificate: false,
      who: "small companies",
      headline: "DLA is keeping this one for small companies.",
      body: "There is no certificate to get. Each order has its own size limit. You say you are under it when you quote.",
    };
  }

  // A SET-ASIDE WE HOLD NO RULING FOR. We know DLA has reserved this order and
  // we do not know for whom. `needsCertificate: true` closes the send block —
  // the safe direction — and `who` is null, so nothing prints a group we have
  // not identified.
  return {
    needsCertificate: true,
    who: null,
    headline: "Not clear who DLA is keeping this one for.",
    body: "DLA is saving this order for one kind of company. The source data does not make clear which kind. Some kinds need a certificate first. Read the order paper before you spend time on it.",
  };
}

/**
 * The second half of what a set-aside asks of a RESELLER.
 *
 * Under the nonmanufacturer rule, 13 CFR 121.406(b), a small business that did
 * not make the item may supply it on a set-aside only if it takes ownership and
 * supplies the product of a small United States manufacturer — unless SBA has
 * waived the rule for that item. Amira does not make loop clamps. The sourcing
 * help two sections down says "Search this number to find who sells it", which
 * routes her to whichever distributor is cheapest, and that product is usually a
 * large manufacturer's. The result is a non-responsive quote, or an award she
 * cannot lawfully fill.
 *
 * It belongs at the moment she goes shopping, not on the set-aside box — so it
 * is its own sentence rather than a fourth clause in setAsideNote().
 */
export function resellerRule(o: MicroOrder, now: Date | null): string | null {
  if (setAsideNote(o) == null) return null;
  // THE VALUE GATE, WHICH THE PRD ALWAYS HAD AND THE BUILD NEVER DID.
  // 13 CFR 121.406(c): the nonmanufacturer rule does NOT apply to a
  // small-business set-aside valued between the micro-purchase threshold and
  // the simplified acquisition threshold. Without this gate the sentence
  // rendered on every set-aside, telling her a part must be American-made when
  // the regulation does not require it — a RESTRICTIVE error, and those cost
  // her the order rather than an afternoon. PRD §4.8 specifies three branches
  // and the build shipped one.
  //
  // Abstain when the value is not computable: we cannot say which side of the
  // threshold an order sits on if we do not know what it is worth, and stating
  // the rule anyway is the same restrictive guess in a different place.
  const v = orderValue(o, now);
  if (v == null) return null;
  if (v > MICRO_PURCHASE_LIMIT) return null;
  return "If you buy this part to resell, a small United States company must make it. This rule does not cover every part. Read the order paper before you buy.";
}

export type OpenVerdict = {
  /** Is there a condition she must meet first? Drives the icon: a condition is
   *  a warning, not a green check. */
  conditional: boolean;
  headline: string;
  body: string;
  term: string;
};

/** The order page's "Can you bid this one?" cell. */
export type BidCell = {
  /** The short answer, e.g. "You can bid this one" or "Not open to you". */
  verdict: string;
  /** True only when DLA's codes let a newcomer supply the part AND no set-aside
   *  narrows it — the one case the cell may mark with a green tick. */
  openToHer: boolean;
  /** A second line, only where the two tests disagree. */
  hint?: string;
};

/**
 * THE CELL MUST NEVER SAY YES WHERE THE VERDICT SAYS "NOT CLEAR".
 *
 * It read `!(codesKnown(o) && !mayBid(o))` as "she can bid", so an order whose
 * acquisition code never reached the record — or reached it with no ruling —
 * showed a green "You can bid this one" directly above Hunter's "Not clear if
 * you can bid this one." Only `mayBid()` says yes; a gap `ourGap()` names is
 * "not clear"; a known refusal is "not open". A set-aside narrows a yes the
 * way `setAsideNote()` words it.
 */
export function bidCell(o: MicroOrder): BidCell {
  if (ourGap(o) != null) {
    return { verdict: "Not clear from DLA's codes", openToHer: false };
  }
  // THE FOURTH ANSWER, AND IT IS STILL A NO. DLA competes this order among the
  // sellers it already has, so "Not open to you" is true and says less than the
  // record does. `openToHer` stays false: we hold no member's CAGE code, so
  // nothing here can say whether she is one of those sellers.
  if (restrictionOf(o)?.route === "approved") {
    return { verdict: "Approved sellers only", openToHer: false };
  }
  // THE FIFTH ANSWER, AND IT IS STILL A NO. "Qualified" is the regulation's own
  // word for the list 2-201.2(t) limits the competition to, and it has to read
  // as a different verdict from "Approved sellers only" one tier up — the two
  // tiers end in different places and a member meeting both must see that in
  // the cell. `openToHer` stays false: no profile here holds her CAGE code, so
  // nothing can say she is on that list.
  if (restrictionOf(o)?.route === "qpl") {
    return { verdict: "Qualified sellers only", openToHer: false };
  }
  if (!mayBid(o)) return { verdict: "Not open to you", openToHer: false };
  const setAside = setAsideNote(o);
  if (setAside == null) return { verdict: "You can bid this one", openToHer: true };
  return {
    verdict:
      setAside.who == null
        ? "Not open to everyone"
        : setAside.needsCertificate
          ? `Only ${setAside.who}`
          : "Small companies only",
    openToHer: false,
    // The acquisition code is open and the set-aside is what stops her: the
    // one case a single line cannot carry.
    hint: "The part is open to any seller. It is the set-aside that narrows it.",
  };
}

/**
 * "May I bid it?" — the verdict, for an order the acquisition codes permit.
 *
 * The codes answer HOW DLA buys the part. They do not answer WHO may offer.
 * mayBid() reads amc/amsc only, so on mp-006 — WOSBC — the page printed the
 * set-aside warning and then, two inches beneath it, "You can bid this one. DLA
 * lets anyone quote this part." Under FAR 19.14 and 13 CFR 128.400 only an
 * SBA-certified woman-owned small business may offer on that
 * order, so the strongest affirmative signal on the surface was false as a
 * matter of law — and set-asides are 50.5% of live DIBBS orders, so it is the
 * ordinary case, not an edge.
 *
 * A set-aside therefore OWNS the verdict whenever there is one. The sentence
 * "DLA lets anyone quote this part" can no longer reach an order that has one.
 */
export function openVerdict(o: MicroOrder): OpenVerdict | null {
  if (!mayBid(o)) return null;
  const sa = setAsideNote(o);
  const gated = o.needsDrawings === true;
  // NULL IS NOT FALSE, AND IT IS NOT THE VERDICT'S JOB EITHER.
  //
  // `dibbs_tech_docs_flag` is true on about a third of mature live records and
  // ABSENT on the rest — it is never false (corpus header). So `=== true` reads
  // every unknown as "no drawings needed", and the caveat that fixed it rode in
  // the verdict headline on nearly two thirds of open orders. A warning that
  // common is wallpaper: she learns to read past it, and then reads past the
  // third where drawings genuinely ARE required.
  //
  // So the verdict answers the one question it exists to answer — may she bid —
  // and the unknown is stated in "Before you quote", where every other
  // thing-to-check already lives and where a reader goes looking. That block
  // reads `o.needsDrawings == null` from the record directly, so this file does
  // not export a flag nothing here uses. Dropping the fact instead would be a
  // silent disqualification (`D-C5`), which is not available.

  const headline = sa
    ? sa.needsCertificate
      ? `Only ${sa.who} can bid this one.`
      : "Any small company can bid this one."
    : gated
      ? "You can bid this one, but you need the drawings first."
      : "You can bid this one.";

  // ONE STRING, ONE OWNER. This branch used to RE-WRITE setAsideNote()'s body
  // instead of composing it, the way the headline already composes `sa.who` —
  // and the copy drifted the moment setAsideNote() was corrected. The clause it
  // dropped is the one that makes the sentence true: the size test is taken
  // PER SOLICITATION against THAT order's size standard (FAR 52.219-1), not
  // once in SAM. So the corrected sentence rendered ONLY where this function
  // returns null — the set-aside block is gated on `openVerdict() == null` —
  // and the truncated one rendered on every SBA set-aside she can actually bid.
  // SBA is four of five live set-asides, so the wrong half was the ordinary case.
  const who = sa ? sa.body : "DLA lets anyone quote this part.";

  const drawings = gated
    ? // "and that takes time" against the drawings section's "wait weeks" — the
      // last daylight between the two owners of this fact, and vagueness where
      // we hold the number (§5: simplifying removes unfamiliar words, never the
      // timeframe). The verdict now states the same duration the section does.
      " To see the drawings you need approval, and that takes weeks."
    : "";

  return {
    conditional: sa?.needsCertificate === true || gated,
    headline,
    body: `${who}${drawings}`,
    // ONE VOCABULARY FOR ONE FIELD, finishing the D4-5 sweep. That decision
    // unified the code chip to `Acquisition code · ${amc}${amsc}` because three
    // names for one field is the acronym rule run badly; it was applied to all
    // four `code` slots in restrictionOf() and NOT here, so an open order page
    // still read "Open competition · 1Z" while the restricted page read
    // "Acquisition code · 1C" — the same field, two vocabularies, on the two
    // surfaces she compares most often (`D-F5`, `D-E4`).
    //
    // "Open competition" was also our own gloss, not an official term: it
    // ascribes a FAR 6.1 full-and-open finding to `Z`, which is the
    // commercial-item code. This slot's job (§4.4) is the term she will meet on
    // DIBBS, and the openness is already stated in the headline above it.
    term:
      (sa ? `Set-aside · ${o.setAside} · ` : "") +
      codeChip(o) +
      (gated ? " · drawings needed" : ""),
  };
}

// One chip, one vocabulary. `Restricted source · 1C`, `Acquisition code · 1D`
// and `Method 3C` were three shapes for ONE field — the acquisition code pair —
// and the reader has to learn all three to know they name the same thing. Which
// axis restricts her is already carried by the headline and the body directly
// above the chip; the chip's job is the official term she will meet on DIBBS.
const codeChip = (o: MicroOrder) => `Acquisition code · ${o.amc}${o.amsc}`;

/** We read the code and hold no ruling for it.
 *
 *  ONE CALLER SINCE 10 SEPTEMBER 2026. It had two: the letter we have never
 *  ruled on, and an open letter under a method we had never ruled on. The
 *  second was `3Z`, and `closedByMethod()` now rules it, so the branch that
 *  shared this object is gone rather than kept as a second entry point. */
const unruledRestriction = (o: MicroOrder): Restriction => ({
  fixable: false,
  route: "unknown",
  code: codeChip(o),
  headline: "Not clear who may sell this one.",
  // No route named, no refusal either, and no invented meaning. She gets one
  // next step and it is a human who knows this. Sentence owned by mentor.ts.
  body: `No clear meaning for this DLA code at source. ${ASK_MENTOR_FIRST}`,
});

/**
 * A restriction we can rule on and can offer no route out of.
 *
 * ROUTE "none" — "Skip this one." is the honest advice on a restriction the
 * primary source describes and gives no way out of, and the order page prints
 * `SKIP_THIS_ONE` off this field. So it is a member-facing choice, not a sort
 * key.
 *
 * ONE BUILDER, THREE CALLERS, and the headline and body are the callers' own.
 * The three dead ends this file names — the letter with no route, the
 * commercial part bought from one supplier, and the part one supplier alone can
 * fill — differ in what they SAY and agree on what they ARE. Building the same
 * four fields three times is how three branches with one meaning drift.
 */
const deadEnd = (o: MicroOrder, headline: string, body: string): Restriction => ({
  fixable: false,
  route: "none",
  code: codeChip(o),
  headline,
  body,
});

/** The sentence for a letter we can rule on and can offer no route for.
 *  `null` when we hold no such ruling — which is not the same as "there is a
 *  route", and `restrictionOf()` keeps the two apart. */
function noRouteReason(o: MicroOrder): string | null {
  const a = o.amsc?.trim().toUpperCase();
  if (a == null || !Object.hasOwn(NO_ROUTE_REASONS, a)) return null;
  return NO_ROUTE_REASONS[a];
}

/**
 * DLA's two codes for this order contradict each other.
 *
 * A CLAIM ABOUT THE RECORD, NOT ABOUT HER PERMISSION. We can read both halves,
 * and PGI 217.75 Exhibit I says they cannot both be true of one part — so one
 * of them is wrong and we cannot tell which. Reading a verdict off either half
 * would be picking one at random and calling it DLA's ruling.
 *
 * `route: "unknown"` because the next step is the same human the unruled letters
 * reach, and `AskMentor` is already on every order page this row opens. The
 * headline and body are its own, so `/small-orders/restricted` groups these
 * apart from "I am not sure who may sell this one." — there we read the codes
 * and hold no ruling, here the codes disagree with each other. Two facts, two
 * groups, two sentences, the same rule that keeps `NO_CODE_REASON` separate.
 *
 * Hunter's held-back ledger counts it on the "have a rule I do not know" line,
 * with the unruled letters. That line is the closest true thing the ledger says:
 * we cannot tell which rule applies to this order. A line of its own would be a
 * better sentence and it is a change to what the board renders, so it is
 * REPORTED rather than taken here.
 */
const dataErrorRestriction = (o: MicroOrder): Restriction => ({
  fixable: false,
  route: "unknown",
  code: codeChip(o),
  headline: "DLA's two codes for this order do not go together.",
  body: `One code says how DLA buys this part. The other says why it is restricted. This pair is not allowed, so one of the two is wrong. ${ASK_MENTOR_FIRST}`,
});

/**
 * The method closes an order whose letter we can read. Two tiers, or `null`.
 *
 * **THE ONE CASE THIS SURFACE UNDERSTOOD AND STILL ABSTAINED ON.** Both tiers
 * were "I am not sure who may sell this one." until this feature, and on a live
 * 500-row board page taken 10 September 2026 they held 56 of the 60 orders in
 * that group. The record was not unclear. We had simply never read the two
 * codes TOGETHER for anything except an affirmative.
 *
 * **IT IS REACHED ONLY WITH AMC 3, 4 OR 5.** `restrictionOf()` runs
 * `codesKnown()` first, which bounds the method to 1-5, so `!methodIsOpen(o)`
 * here means exactly the three methods PGI 217.75 2-201.1 describes as buying
 * from one named company: (d) "Acquire, for the second or subsequent time,
 * directly from the actual manufacturer", (e) the same for the first time, and
 * (f) "Acquire directly from a sole source contractor which is not the actual
 * manufacturer".
 *
 * **NEITHER SENTENCE SAYS "THE MAKER".** AMC 5 is explicitly a supplier that is
 * NOT the manufacturer, and the live cut carries `5Z`. "One supplier" is the
 * claim all three methods support, so it is the claim both sentences make.
 *
 * **BOTH ARE DEAD ENDS FOR THIS ORDER, NOT FOR THE PART FOREVER.** 2-202(d)
 * allows a one-time buy by another method without recoding, and 2-203(a) says a
 * code "should never be considered fixed with respect to either technical
 * circumstance or time". Neither sentence claims otherwise; both describe the
 * order in front of her.
 *
 * @returns the restriction the method produces, or `null` when it produces none
 */
function closedByMethod(o: MicroOrder): Restriction | null {
  if (methodIsOpen(o)) return null;

  // THE OPEN LETTER, CLOSED BY ITS NUMBER — its own tier and its own sentence,
  // because she can be told exactly what is going on. Z reaches this alone: G
  // is the other member of `OPEN_SUFFIXES` and 2-201.2(g) allows it beside AMC
  // 1 or 2 only, so `3G` is a data error and never arrives here.
  //
  // 2-201.2(z): "This part is a commercial product, nondevelopmental item, or
  // commercially available off-the-shelf item ... If one source is available,
  // AMCs 3, 4, or 5 are valid." Both halves are said below, in that order,
  // because the first is what makes the second surprising.
  if (suffixIsOpen(o)) {
    return deadEnd(
      o,
      "DLA is buying this commercial part from one supplier.",
      "This part is sold as a commercial product. But DLA's code says one supplier is available for this order, so DLA is buying from that supplier. It is not asking other sellers for a price.",
    );
  }

  // THE ELEVEN LETTERS WHOSE OWN PARAGRAPH SAYS ONE SUPPLIER. The sentence
  // repeats the clause and nothing else — see `SINGLE_SUPPLIER_SUFFIXES` for
  // each letter's wording and for why one sentence covers all eleven.
  const suffix = o.amsc?.trim().toUpperCase();
  if (suffix != null && SINGLE_SUPPLIER_SUFFIXES.includes(suffix)) {
    return deadEnd(
      o,
      "Only one supplier can fill this one.",
      "DLA's code says one supplier is available for this part. DLA is buying from that supplier and is not asking other sellers for a price.",
    );
  }
  return null;
}

/**
 * DLA competes this order among the sellers it already has. Or `null`.
 *
 * **IT IS REACHED ONLY WITH AMC 1 OR 2.** `methodIsOpen()` is tested here
 * rather than left to the caller's order, for the reason `closedByMethod()`
 * tests `!methodIsOpen()`: the claim this tier makes is a claim about a
 * COMPETITIVE buy, and 2-201.1(b) and (c) are the only two paragraphs that
 * describe one — "Suitable for competitive acquisition for the second or
 * subsequent time" and "for the first time". Under AMC 3, 4 or 5 the same
 * letter means one supplier and `closedByMethod()` already owns it.
 *
 * **THE HEADLINE IS NOT THE SOURCE APPROVAL REQUEST HEADLINE.** "Only approved
 * sellers can quote this one." belongs to C, whose paragraph names the
 * procedure an alternate source qualifies under. A member meeting both states
 * has to be able to tell them apart at a glance, so this one names the actor
 * and the act: DLA is asking, and it is asking the sellers it already has.
 *
 * **IT IS A DEAD END FOR THIS ORDER, NOT FOR THE PART FOREVER.** 2-202(d)
 * allows a one-time buy by another method without recoding, 2-203(a) says a
 * code "should never be considered fixed with respect to either technical
 * circumstance or time", and (q) itself says breakout to competition "is
 * expected to be achieved". The sentence describes the order in front of her.
 *
 * @returns the restriction the pair produces, or `null` when it produces none
 */
function approvedSourcesOnly(o: MicroOrder): Restriction | null {
  if (!methodIsOpen(o)) return null;
  const suffix = o.amsc?.trim().toUpperCase();
  // `Object.hasOwn`, not a bare lookup: `amsc` arrives from the DIBBS feed, and
  // every table keyed by it follows the rule `units.ts` set.
  if (suffix == null || !Object.hasOwn(APPROVED_SOURCE_REASONS, suffix)) return null;
  return {
    fixable: false,
    route: "approved",
    code: codeChip(o),
    headline: "DLA is only asking the sellers it already has for this one.",
    body: APPROVED_SOURCE_REASONS[suffix],
  };
}

/**
 * DLA buys this order off a qualified products list. Or `null`.
 *
 * **IT IS REACHED ONLY WITH AMC 1 OR 2**, for the reason `approvedSourcesOnly()`
 * tests the same thing: the claim is about a COMPETITIVE buy, and 2-201.1(b)
 * and (c) are the only two paragraphs that describe one. 2-201.2(t) authorises
 * no other method, so `pairIsImpossible()` has already called a `3T` record a
 * data error before this function runs. The test here is belt and braces on a
 * claim that may never be made off a pair the regulation rejects.
 *
 * **THE HEADLINE IS NEITHER OF THE OTHER TWO.** "Only approved sellers can quote
 * this one." is C's, and C ends in a Source Approval Request. "DLA is only
 * asking the sellers it already has for this one." is Q's, and Q ends in no
 * route at all. This one names the list, because the list IS the route and it
 * is the only one of the three a member can work toward on her own evidence.
 *
 * **THE BODY STATES THE ROUTE AND THEN STATES ITS COST.** (t) allows two kinds
 * of seller — listed, or qualified for listing at the time of award — so both
 * are said. Getting qualified means the government examines the part first, and
 * that runs past this order's closing date. Saying only the first half would be
 * an invitation to spend an afternoon on a quote she cannot win; saying only the
 * second half would hide a route she can take for later orders. No timescale is
 * named, because we hold none.
 *
 * **NO SOURCE APPROVAL REQUEST, AND NOT BECAUSE THE FORM IS HARD.** (c) names
 * the design control activity's own qualification procedure and (t) names a
 * qualified products list. They are different procedures under different parts
 * of the regulation, and this file's header calls sending her to a request that
 * cannot succeed worse than saying nothing.
 *
 * @returns the restriction the pair produces, or `null` when it produces none
 */
function qualifiedListOnly(o: MicroOrder): Restriction | null {
  if (!methodIsOpen(o)) return null;
  const suffix = o.amsc?.trim().toUpperCase();
  if (suffix == null || !QPL_SUFFIXES.includes(suffix)) return null;
  return {
    fixable: false,
    route: "qpl",
    code: codeChip(o),
    headline: "Only sellers on DLA's qualified list can quote this one.",
    // The term is explained on first use, then used. The order page renders
    // 2-201.2(t) verbatim beneath this — "see FAR Part 9 and DFARS Part 209"
    // and all — so the regulation is cited in the government's own words and
    // this sentence does not cite it a second time in weaker ones.
    body: `A qualified products list names the sellers DLA may buy this part from. DLA buys only from a seller on that list, or from one qualified for listing by the time it awards. A seller gets on the list by qualifying the part with the government first. That takes longer than this order allows, so it is a step toward later orders. ${ASK_MENTOR_FIRST}`,
  };
}

/**
 * How many companies DLA's own record names as approved to supply this part.
 *
 * **A FACT ABOUT THE RECORD, AND NEVER A RULING.** `approvedSources` reaches us
 * through the `nsn_records` join, the same way `unitOfIssue` does, and it says
 * who DLA has cleared for the PART. It says nothing about the member: no
 * profile on this surface holds her CAGE code, so no count here can tell her
 * whether she is one of them. Every sentence built on this must name the number
 * and stop there.
 *
 * COUNTED ON DISTINCT CAGE CODES, because a company is what is being counted. A
 * supplier approved for two part numbers is two rows on `SourcingPanel` and one
 * company here. A row with no CAGE code names no company and is not counted —
 * the raw `partNumbers` string is a part number, not a supplier.
 */
export function approvedSourceCount(o: MicroOrder): number {
  const cages = new Set<string>();
  for (const source of o.approvedSources) {
    const cage = (source.cage ?? "").trim().toUpperCase();
    if (cage !== "") cages.add(cage);
  }
  return cages.size;
}

/**
 * The one line the order page carries over DLA's approved-source list, or
 * `null` when the record names no company.
 *
 * It renders BESIDE whatever verdict the order has, including "not clear from
 * DLA's codes" — a count of approved companies is the government's record and
 * is the same for every member, so a permission verdict does not gate it. The
 * words state the count and make no claim about her: "DLA has approved 4
 * companies" is checkable; "you are not one of them" is not.
 */
export function approvedSourceNote(o: MicroOrder): string | null {
  const n = approvedSourceCount(o);
  if (n === 0) return null;
  return `DLA has approved ${n} ${n === 1 ? "company" : "companies"} to supply this part.`;
}

export function restrictionOf(o: MicroOrder): Restriction | null {
  // Unknown codes are NOT a restriction. Saying "only the maker can supply
  // this" when we simply could not read the code is a confident false claim.
  if (!codesKnown(o)) return null;

  // THE PAIR FIRST, AND ONLY TO CALL THE RECORD WRONG. A combination Exhibit I
  // rejects is not a harder restriction; it is a record that cannot be true, so
  // no verdict may be read off either half of it. `1D` and `2D` are the live
  // case — 2-201.2(d) allows AMCs 3, 4 and 5 only. Line 29 of this file records
  // the `1C` / `3C` version of the same confusion, where a pair produced two
  // opposite rulings; the difference is that `1C` is a lawful pair we were
  // reading twice and `1D` is not a lawful pair at all.
  if (pairIsImpossible(o)) {
    return dataErrorRestriction(o);
  }

  // SUFFIX FIRST. The letter is the sourced axis and it decides; the method
  // number is never a refusal (see the header). `3C` and `1C` now read the same
  // sentence, because they are the same restriction.
  //
  // Restricted by the SUFFIX. Two outcomes, and the difference is whether a form
  // exists. Telling her a Source Approval Request fixes a P or an R would send
  // her to spend weeks on paperwork that cannot succeed — the Government does
  // not hold the rights to add a source.
  if (isSarFixable(o)) {
    return {
      fixable: true,
      route: "sar",
      code: codeChip(o),
      headline: "Only approved sellers can quote this one.",
      // "it is worth it if you sell a lot of these" named the payoff and left out
      // the entry condition. A Source Approval Request is decided on evidence —
      // prior production of the identical item for the Government or the design
      // control activity, or a complete technical data package — not on volume.
      // A first-time reseller with neither cannot win one, and this file's own
      // header says telling her to file a request she cannot win is worse than
      // saying nothing. This tier is now C alone: D left on 19 August 2026 and
      // B left on 10 September 2026, both on `SAR_FIXABLE_SUFFIXES`' reasons.
      // The share figures those departures were once quoted against came from a
      // research file this repository no longer holds, so they are not restated
      // here — `relevance.acquisitionCodes.test.ts` measures the tier instead.
      // No "skip" sentence: a restriction with a real route must offer the
      // route, and the entry condition is the honest signal she can apply
      // herself without being told to walk away.
      body: "The form is called a Source Approval Request. DLA only approves sellers who have made or supplied this part before. The full drawings also work.",
    };
  }
  // RESTRICTED, WITH NO ROUTE WE CAN NAME — and the reason is the letter's own.
  // B, D, P and R each carry their own sentence out of `NO_ROUTE_REASONS`,
  // because the last time this branch shared one sentence across a letter set
  // it told S, V and Y a data-rights fact that is untrue of all three.
  const noRoute = noRouteReason(o);
  if (noRoute != null) {
    return deadEnd(o, "DLA is not taking new sellers for this one.", noRoute);
  }
  // THE LETTER IS RULED AND THE METHOD CLOSES THE ORDER — the commercial part
  // bought from one supplier, and the part only one supplier can fill. Both
  // used to fall through to "I am not sure who may sell this one." over a
  // record that says who. `closedByMethod()` carries the sourcing.
  //
  // IT SITS AFTER THE THREE LETTER RULINGS AND BEFORE THE ABSTENTION, and both
  // sides of that matter. A letter we can rule on alone must keep its own
  // sentence whatever the method is, which is the "the letter rules" decision
  // in this file's header. A letter we cannot rule on must not reach the mentor
  // when the number has already answered her question.
  const closed = closedByMethod(o);
  if (closed != null) {
    return closed;
  }
  // LIMITED COMPETITION, WHICH IS A RULING AND NOT AN ABSTENTION. It sits after
  // `closedByMethod()` so the same letter beside AMC 3, 4 or 5 keeps the
  // one-supplier sentence its own paragraph earns, and before the abstention so
  // a letter 2-201.2(g) names does not reach a mentor with the answer in hand.
  const approved = approvedSourcesOnly(o);
  if (approved != null) {
    return approved;
  }
  // THE SECOND LIMITED-COMPETITION TIER, AND THE ONE WITH A ROUTE. It sits
  // beside `approvedSourcesOnly()` rather than inside it: 2-201.2(t) names a
  // qualified products list and 2-201.2(q) names no procedure at all, so one
  // sentence over both would tell a T order there is no way in. Order between
  // the two is free — no letter is in both sets — and they are written in the
  // order the tiers were built.
  const qualified = qualifiedListOnly(o);
  if (qualified != null) {
    return qualified;
  }
  if (isUnverifiedSuffix(o)) {
    return unruledRestriction(o);
  }
  return null;
}

/**
 * What we say about an order whose acquisition code we could not read at all.
 *
 * NOT a `restrictionOf()` branch, and it must not become one: that function is
 * right to return `null` here — an unread code is not a restriction, and
 * `liveCorpus.heldBackLedger.test.ts` pins the refusal: over the live corpus it
 * asserts every named-refusal row has readable codes and every `unreadable` row
 * does not. But "not a restriction" is not
 * "open", and `queryOrders()` put these rows in neither list, so the board
 * counted them ("2 do not say who may sell them") and offered one destination
 * that dropped them. They rendered on no screen in the product while their own
 * order page carried a designed state and the mentor control built for exactly
 * this tier. `MicroResult.unreadable` is the pool; this is what the destination
 * says over it, and it is shaped as a `Restriction` so the restricted page
 * groups it the same way as the other three with no second code path.
 *
 * Worded off `heldBack()`'s own line, deliberately: the line she taps from and
 * the group she lands on say the same thing about the same orders (`D-E4`).
 * Distinct from the `unknown` route — there we READ the code and hold no ruling
 * for it; here no code reached us. Two facts, two groups, two sentences, and the
 * ledger already keeps them on separate lines for the same reason.
 *
 * `route: "unknown"` because the next step is the same one: a human looks
 * first, and `AskMentor` is on the order page every one of these rows opens.
 * `code: ""` because there is no code to print — the row renders no chip rather
 * than "Acquisition code · nullnull".
 */
export const NO_CODE_REASON: Restriction = {
  fixable: false,
  route: "unknown",
  code: "",
  headline: "No acquisition code available at source.",
  body: `That code says who may sell the part. ${ASK_MENTOR_FIRST}`,
};

/**
 * Which of OUR two shortcomings stopped us ruling on this order, or `null`.
 *
 * **THE DISTINCTION THIS FILE ALREADY MADE AND DID NOT ACT ON.** `heldBack()`
 * withheld an order on four grounds and only one of them is a fact about the
 * order. `ruled` is DLA's restriction — we read the codes and they refuse her,
 * and withholding that saves her an afternoon. The two below are ours:
 *
 *   · `unread`  — no code reached us. A DATA gap. `codesKnown()` is false.
 *   · `unruled` — a code reached us and we hold no ruling we can apply to it.
 *                 A KNOWLEDGE gap. `restrictionOf()`'s `unknown` route.
 *
 * Presenting either as a reason she may not bid states our shortcoming as
 * DLA's restriction. So an order carrying one goes on the main list, and
 * `queryOrders()` reads THIS function to decide that.
 *
 * **IT IS NOT `mayBid()` AND IT MAY NEVER BECOME IT.** `mayBid()` answers
 * "does DLA permit her to sell this part" and it is unchanged by this feature —
 * `relevance.acquisitionCodes.test.ts` enumerates all 196 method x suffix pairs
 * and pins it to `1G`, `1Z`, `2G`, `2Z`. This function answers "may she LOOK at
 * it", which is a different question with a different answer. Conflating the
 * two is how a member is told she may bid something she may not.
 *
 * **THE IMPOSSIBLE PAIR TRAVELS WITH `unruled`**, because `restrictionOf()`
 * gives it the same `unknown` route: we can read both codes and Exhibit I says
 * they cannot both be true, so we hold no ruling to apply. It is not evidence
 * against her either. DH-32 reports that the ledger line over these two facts
 * would read better split, and this function does not take that decision.
 *
 * **THE `approved` AND `qpl` ROUTES ARE NOT GAPS.** Each is a ruling we can
 * state and cite, so both return `null` here and their orders leave the unruled
 * pool they sat in — 1Q and 2Q, 74 open orders, and 1T and 2T, 482 open orders,
 * of the 695 unruled measured on the live board on 2026-09-16.
 * `eligibleByCode()` below is what keeps both rulings out of the For you tab.
 */
export type OurGap = "unread" | "unruled";
export function ourGap(o: MicroOrder): OurGap | null {
  if (!codesKnown(o)) return "unread";
  return restrictionOf(o)?.route === "unknown" ? "unruled" : null;
}

/**
 * May she bid this order by its acquisition code? The "For you" tab's gate
 * (owner, 2026-09-15): an order DLA refuses her, or one we cannot rule on, is
 * not shown there. `ourGap()` is checked beside `mayBid()` so an order we hold
 * no ruling for can never pass, whatever the two tables say later. Set-asides
 * are not part of this gate.
 *
 * **THE `approved` AND `qpl` TIERS ARE OUT, AND `mayBid()` IS WHAT HOLDS THEM
 * OUT.** Neither Q nor T is in `OPEN_SUFFIXES`, so `mayBid()` is false on every
 * pair `approvedSourcesOnly()` and `qualifiedListOnly()` rule, and this function
 * cannot return true for one. That is structural rather than a second test, and
 * `relevance.acquisitionCodes.test.ts` pins it: nothing tells us whether the
 * member is one of the sellers DLA has approved, or one of the sellers on its
 * qualified products list, so an order she probably cannot win must not sit in
 * the tab that promises she can bid.
 */
export function eligibleByCode(o: MicroOrder): boolean {
  return mayBid(o) && ourGap(o) == null;
}

/**
 * What we say about an order we put on her board without ruling on it.
 *
 * **THE NOTE IS NOT A VERDICT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE RISK OF THIS TIER.** "I
 * could not rule on this one" is true. "You may bid this one" is not, and no
 * sentence here may imply it — which is why every note ends by saying, in
 * words, that it is not that claim. `openVerdict()` still owns the affirmative
 * and still returns `null` on every order this function speaks about.
 *
 * **THE TWO GAPS KEEP DIFFERENT SENTENCES.** "No code for this part reached
 * me" and "DLA's code reached me and I hold no ruling for it" are different
 * facts, and one sentence over both would tell her we looked and failed on an
 * order we never looked at. Same rule that keeps `NO_CODE_REASON` apart from
 * `unruledRestriction()` one screen over.
 *
 * `card` is the sentence the board row carries and it is the headline the order
 * page prints, read from the tier that owns it rather than written twice — the
 * row she taps from and the page she lands on say the same thing (`D-E4`).
 */
export type GapNote = {
  /** Which gap: no code reached us, or no ruling for the code that did. */
  gap: OurGap;
  /** The one line the board row carries. Owned by the tier, not written here. */
  card: string;
  /** The note her order page carries under the verdict. */
  body: string;
};

/** The board row and the order-page note for an order we could not rule on. */
export function gapNote(o: MicroOrder): GapNote | null {
  const gap = ourGap(o);
  if (gap === null) return null;
  const ruling = restrictionOf(o);
  // WHOSE GAP, THEN WHAT IT IS NOT. Two questions, two sentences.
  //
  // IT WAS FIVE SENTENCES AND THREE OF THEM SAID ONE THING. The note opened
  // "This one is on your board because I could not rule on it." — which is the
  // verdict's own headline, one line above it, reworded. Then "No code for this
  // part reached me." restated the verdict's body, which already says "I could
  // not read the code that says who may supply the part." Since the verdict and
  // the price became ONE card, all of that stacks in a single amber block and
  // the repetition is impossible to miss.
  //
  // The cause sentence stays — it is the only thing that tells `unread` and
  // `unruled` apart, and a member who reads the note alone must still learn
  // which gap this is. What went is the opener, which was the verdict again,
  // and the split between "not a rule about you" and its own full stop.
  const OURS = "This is a gap in the source data, not a rule about you.";
  const NOT_PERMISSION = "That does not mean you may bid this one.";
  return gap === "unread"
    ? {
        gap,
        card: NO_CODE_REASON.headline,
        // NO CAUSE SENTENCE ON THIS ARM. It read "No code for this part
        // reached me." directly under a verdict body already saying "I could
        // not read the code that says who may supply the part." — one fact,
        // two sentences, forty pixels apart, in one amber card since the
        // verdict and the price were merged.
        // The `unruled` arm below KEEPS its cause, because there the verdict
        // states DLA's restriction rather than our gap, so "a code reached me
        // and I hold no ruling for it" is the only place that is said.
        body: `${OURS} ${NOT_PERMISSION}`,
      }
    : {
        gap,
        // Non-null by construction: `ourGap()` returns `unruled` only where
        // `restrictionOf()` returned a route, so the fallback is unreachable.
        // It is here because a headline is what the row renders, and an empty
        // row is worse than the abstention it would be standing in for.
        card: ruling?.headline ?? NO_CODE_REASON.headline,
        body: `The record has DLA's code, but not what it means. ${OURS} ${NOT_PERMISSION}`,
      };
}

// ── Stage 2 · capability ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

/**
 * The mark an over-cap row carries on the board, or `null` inside the cap.
 *
 * ONE OWNER FOR THE WORDS AND FOR THE RULING. The card asks this; the ledger
 * counts `overMicroCap()`; there is no second reading of `orderValue()` against
 * the limit anywhere. It states the fact and gives no instruction — the order
 * page owns what she does about it, and a micro-purchase cap is a rule about
 * the buying method, not a refusal by DLA.
 */
export function overCapNote(o: MicroOrder, now: Date | null): string | null {
  return overMicroCap(o, now) ? OVER_CAP_NOTE : null;
}

/** The words, once. `usd()` writes the limit, so the figure cannot drift from
 *  `MICRO_PURCHASE_LIMIT`. */
export const OVER_CAP_NOTE = `Over the ${usd(MICRO_PURCHASE_LIMIT)} limit`;

/**
 * Over the $15,000 cap — INCLUDING on a price we will not stand behind.
 *
 * `orderValue()` withdraws a total derived from a stale price, deliberately: it
 * is a figure she acts on (`D-A2`). This is not that figure. It is a boolean
 * nobody sees, and the two questions are different: "what is this worth" has no
 * honest answer on a stale price, but "is this over the cap" does — the last
 * price DLA paid says $45,238, and $45,238 was over the cap in 2023 too.
 *
 * Reading `orderValue()` alone let a stale-priced order over the cap sit on a
 * board whose own accounting says the cap holds those back, and kept it out of
 * the "N are bigger than $15,000" line. ONE predicate decides every reader —
 * the mark on the card and the count beside it — so the two cannot disagree.
 *
 * SINCE 2026-09-16 IT HIDES NOTHING. It used to drop the row; now it marks it.
 * An order we cannot price AT ALL is still not judged over the cap, so it
 * carries no mark rather than a guess.
 */
export function overMicroCap(o: MicroOrder, now: Date | null): boolean {
  const v = orderValue(o, now);
  if (v != null) return v > MICRO_PURCHASE_LIMIT;
  const p = pricePoint(o);
  if (p == null || o.quantity == null || o.quantity <= 0) return false;
  return p.amount * o.quantity > MICRO_PURCHASE_LIMIT;
}

export type MicroMemberContext = {
  categories: string[];
  /** Most she can lay out before being paid. null when she has not said. */
  cashCeiling: number | null;
  /** Stock numbers she has delivered before. */
  suppliedNsns: string[];
  /** Stock numbers she has said yes to on the sourcing panel. */
  attestedNsns: string[];
  /** Stock numbers she has said no to on the sourcing panel. They RANK LAST;
   *  they are never removed. See queryOrders(). */
  declinedNsns: string[];
  /** Stock numbers she has WON, with the price she told us she quoted.
   *
   *  The second half of the goal — "keep them winning" — lives here. Without
   *  it, a part she already won at a price she knows comes round again and we
   *  quote DLA's record back at her as if she had never seen it. This is also
   *  the only place her own price is ever read back, which is what makes
   *  capturing it worth asking for (PRD 4.1b, 7.2). */
  wonQuotes: { nsn: string; pricePerUnit: number | null; wonOn: string }[];
  /** Her matching-engine `finalScore` (0–100) per order id, where the engine
   *  scored the order for her. The first source of fit. See `fitScore()`. */
  matchScores: Readonly<Record<string, number>>;
  /** The NAICS codes on her profile. The fallback fit for an unscored order. */
  profileNaics: string[];
  /** Those codes plus the ones the matching engine counts as related. */
  alliedNaics: string[];
};

export const EMPTY_CONTEXT: MicroMemberContext = {
  wonQuotes: [],
  matchScores: {},
  profileNaics: [],
  alliedNaics: [],
  categories: [],
  cashCeiling: null,
  suppliedNsns: [],
  attestedNsns: [],
  declinedNsns: [],
};

export function matchesCategory(o: MicroOrder, m: MicroMemberContext): boolean {
  return m.categories.length === 0 || m.categories.includes(o.category);
}
/** We could not work out what this order is. Not the same as "she does not sell it". */
export function isUncategorised(o: MicroOrder): boolean {
  return o.category === "other";
}
/**
 * Is this order comfortably inside the cash she said she has?
 *
 * RANKING ONLY. This must never remove an order from her board. She can reach
 * a bigger order with supplier terms, a partner, or an invoice — and the whole
 * point of this feature is that she is never quietly disqualified. Hiding work
 * she might be able to finance is the more expensive error.
 */
export function withinCash(o: MicroOrder, m: MicroMemberContext, now: Date | null): boolean {
  if (m.cashCeiling == null) return true;
  const v = orderValue(o, now);
  return v == null || v <= m.cashCeiling;
}

// ── Filters — QUESTIONS with answers, not a row of toggles ────────────────
//
// The first build shipped four independent toggles, and two of them were the
// SAME AXIS: "Small orders only" (under $1,000) and "Bigger jobs too" (over
// $15,000) could both be on at once, which is a state with no meaning. Four
// booleans is sixteen combinations, most of them nonsense, and a toggle row
// tells a member nothing about which choices exclude each other.
//
// A filter is a small set of questions, each with mutually exclusive answers in
// her words. Contradiction is then unrepresentable rather than merely
// discouraged. The house pattern, from Awards: she picks a question, never a
// field and an operator.

export type SizeAnswer = "any" | "under1k" | "1kTo5k" | "over5k";
export type DeliveryAnswer = "any" | "days30" | "days60";

export type MicroFilters = {
  size: SizeAnswer;
  delivery: DeliveryAnswer;
};

export const NO_FILTERS: MicroFilters = { size: "any", delivery: "any" };

export function filtersActive(f: MicroFilters): boolean {
  return f.size !== "any" || f.delivery !== "any";
}

export const SIZE_QUESTION = {
  question: "How big an order?",
  answers: [
    { id: "any" as const, label: "Any size", hint: "" },
    { id: "under1k" as const, label: "Under $1,000", hint: "Good ones to start with" },
    { id: "1kTo5k" as const, label: "$1,000 to $5,000", hint: "" },
    {
      id: "over5k" as const,
      label: "More than $5,000",
      // The $15,000 default cap used to be its own toggle, which is what let
      // the two size controls contradict. That cap is gone entirely since
      // 2026-09-16 — a big order is SHOWN, with a mark — so this answer now
      // does one thing: it narrows to orders over $5,000.
      // Its hint ("Includes orders over $15,000") was removed at the owner's
      // call on 2026-09-15; filterSummary() still names the $15,000 once she
      // picks it.
      hint: "",
    },
  ],
};

export const DELIVERY_QUESTION = {
  question: "How long do you get to deliver?",
  answers: [
    { id: "any" as const, label: "Any", hint: "" },
    { id: "days30" as const, label: "30 days or more", hint: "" },
    { id: "days60" as const, label: "60 days or more", hint: "" },
  ],
  // The hint teaches while it filters — this is the trap that catches newcomers.
  note: "If you do not keep stock, choose more days.",
};

function passesSize(o: MicroOrder, a: SizeAnswer, now: Date | null): boolean {
  if (a === "any") return true;
  const v = orderValue(o, now);
  // An order with no known value cannot honestly satisfy a size band. It stays
  // visible under "Any size" and drops out of the specific ones.
  if (v == null) return false;
  if (a === "under1k") return v < 1000;
  if (a === "1kTo5k") return v >= 1000 && v <= 5000;
  return v > 5000;
}

function passesDelivery(o: MicroOrder, a: DeliveryAnswer): boolean {
  if (a === "any") return true;
  // Unknown is not "generous". A filter promising 60+ days must not include
  // orders whose window we cannot read.
  if (o.deliveryDays == null) return false;
  return o.deliveryDays >= (a === "days60" ? 60 : 30);
}

export function passesFilters(o: MicroOrder, f: MicroFilters, now: Date | null): boolean {
  if (!passesSize(o, f.size, now)) return false;
  if (!passesDelivery(o, f.delivery)) return false;
  return true;
}

/**
 * One plain sentence describing what she is looking at, for the line above the
 * list. A count badge on a gear icon does not answer "am I seeing everything?"
 */
export function filterSummary(f: MicroFilters): string | null {
  if (!filtersActive(f)) return null;

  const size =
    f.size === "under1k"
      ? "orders under $1,000"
      : f.size === "1kTo5k"
        ? "orders between $1,000 and $5,000"
        : f.size === "over5k"
          ? // The band is everything over $5,000, big orders included, and the
            // sentence says so rather than leaving her to assume a ceiling. It
            // named the $15,000 back when this answer LIFTED a default cap; the
            // cap is gone, the clause is still true, and clause 3 exists
            // precisely to answer "am I seeing everything?".
            "orders over $5,000, including big ones over $15,000"
          : null;

  const days = f.delivery === "days30" ? 30 : f.delivery === "days60" ? 60 : null;

  // Delivery on its own needs a noun to attach to, or the sentence reads
  // "Showing with 60 days or more to deliver."
  if (!size && days) {
    return `Showing orders with ${days} days or more to deliver.`;
  }

  const subject = size ?? "orders";

  // Two short sentences beat one long one. All three filters at once ran to
  // fifteen words as a single clause.
  return days
    ? `Showing ${subject}. You get ${days} days or more to deliver.`
    : `Showing ${subject}.`;
}

/**
 * What the board is narrowed to, in words: her categories first, then the
 * filters. Category labels hold commas ("Pipes, valves and fittings"), so more
 * than one is counted rather than listed.
 */
export function narrowedSummary(
  categories: readonly MicroCategory[],
  filters: MicroFilters,
): string {
  const parts =
    categories.length === 0
      ? null
      : categories.length === 1
        ? `Only ${CATEGORY_LABEL[categories[0]].toLowerCase()}.`
        : `Only the ${categories.length} kinds of parts you picked.`;
  return [parts, filterSummary(filters)].filter(Boolean).join(" ");
}

export type MicroSort = "best" | "closing" | "biggest";

// ── Stage 3 · ranking ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Internal only. Never rendered, never converted to a percentage, never shown
// as a "match score". It exists to put the list in an order.

/**
 * Her history as sets, built ONCE per query.
 *
 * relevanceScore used to scan four arrays for every comparison, so the cost of
 * sorting her board grew with the history — the one asset this feature exists
 * to accumulate. 4,000 rows with 2,000 history entries took 1.9 seconds.
 */
export type MemberIndex = {
  won: Map<string, MicroMemberContext["wonQuotes"][number]>;
  supplied: Set<string>;
  attested: Set<string>;
  declined: Set<string>;
};
export function memberIndex(m: MicroMemberContext): MemberIndex {
  return {
    won: new Map(m.wonQuotes.map((q) => [q.nsn, q])),
    supplied: new Set(m.suppliedNsns),
    attested: new Set(m.attestedNsns),
    declined: new Set(m.declinedNsns),
  };
}

/**
 * Can she actually price this one from what DLA paid?
 *
 * ONE test, read by the score below AND by the card's reason line — the two
 * surfaces that were still asking `o.lastPricePerUnit != null` (`D-A5`). The raw
 * field is not the answer to that question: on a stale award the ladder states
 * the figure as history and WITHDRAWS the instruction, and on an all-surplus
 * record it abstains entirely, while `lastPricePerUnit` is populated in both.
 * So the card said "you can price it" beside a block saying "that is too old to
 * price from", and the score paid +50 for a price the product will not stand
 * behind.
 *
 * The ceiling is the right test rather than `pricePoint()`: a ceiling is exactly
 * "there is a number we will put in front of her", which is what "so you can
 * price it" promises. One ceiling, one owner — this file never re-derives it.
 */
function canPriceFromHistory(o: MicroOrder, now: Date | null): boolean {
  // Same ladder, same answer, without building the three strings that carry it:
  // priceAdvice() runs an Intl date format per call, and this is asked once per
  // row on every query. pricing.ts owns both, so the two cannot drift.
  return hasUsableCeiling(o, now);
}

/** Points a perfect fit adds to Best first. Below a part she said she can get
 *  (400), above a year of orders (100): fit ranks among orders she has not
 *  answered for, and never outranks her own answer. */
export const FIT_MAX_POINTS = 200;

/** The fit, on the engine's 0–100 scale, given to an unscored order whose NAICS
 *  code is one on her profile. An APPROXIMATION of what the engine's rule half
 *  gives an exact category fit, so an unscored order does not outrank a scored
 *  one on a code alone. */
export const NAICS_EXACT_FIT = 40;

/** The same, for a code the engine counts as related to one of hers. */
export const NAICS_ALLIED_FIT = 20;

/**
 * How well this order fits her business, 0–100, or null when nothing says.
 *
 * ONE OWNER FOR FIT, AND IT IS THE MATCHING ENGINE. A small order is an
 * opportunity, and the engine scores it for her against her whole profile —
 * NAICS, certifications, place and her capability statement. Its score wins
 * wherever a match row exists. An order posted since the engine's last rescore
 * has none, so it falls back to her NAICS codes: exact first, then related.
 */
export function fitScore(o: MicroOrder, m: MicroMemberContext): number | null {
  const engine = m.matchScores[o.id];
  if (typeof engine === "number" && Number.isFinite(engine)) return engine;
  if (o.naics == null) return null;
  if (m.profileNaics.includes(o.naics)) return NAICS_EXACT_FIT;
  if (m.alliedNaics.includes(o.naics)) return NAICS_ALLIED_FIT;
  return null;
}

export function relevanceScore(
  o: MicroOrder,
  m: MicroMemberContext,
  now: Date | null,
  ix: MemberIndex = memberIndex(m),
): number {
  let s = 0;
  if (ix.won.has(o.nsn)) s += 2000;
  if (ix.supplied.has(o.nsn)) s += 1000;
  if (ix.attested.has(o.nsn)) s += 400;
  const fit = fitScore(o, m);
  if (fit != null) s += Math.round((Math.min(Math.max(fit, 0), 100) / 100) * FIT_MAX_POINTS);
  // The +120 for one or two past winners is GONE. It was the "less competition"
  // inference still running after the sentence that stated it was withdrawn —
  // her board reordered by a belief she could not see, check or disagree with.
  //
  // And the belief is probably backwards. On DIBBS a stock number with only one
  // or two historical awardees is more often one where the commercial channel
  // is thin — single authorised distributor, OEM-controlled, long lead — which
  // is exactly the condition that pushes a first-timer toward surplus stock,
  // the disqualifier this same page warns about. The MANY-winner part is the
  // easier one to source.
  // THREE READS HERE TOO, and this was the last surface holding two.
  //
  // `if (o.repeats)` gave the bonus to `true` and withheld it from `null`,
  // which on a RANKING is not abstention — it is a relative penalty on an
  // order whose paper we could not read, applied for a reason that has nothing
  // to do with her. passesRepeats() one screen up already settled the question
  // for this exact field, with a measured base rate rather than a charity:
  // live `repeats` is true about 95% of the time and never false, so unknown
  // is more likely than not a year of orders. Two functions in one file cannot
  // act on opposite beliefs about the same field.
  //
  // Ranking as-if-true asserts nothing to her: the card still reads "The order
  // paper does not say if it is a year of orders." An unknown is ranked with
  // the group it probably belongs to and labelled honestly on its face.
  //
  // The reason line and the mentor prompt keep testing `=== true`, and that is
  // correct — those SAY something, and abstaining is the right answer when we
  // do not know.
  if (o.repeats !== false) s += 100;
  if (o.fastAward) s += 60;
  // Drawings she cannot see yet are a gate, not a detail. An ungated order
  // outranks a gated one for a member with no approvals — it never hides one.
  if (o.needsDrawings && ix.supplied.size === 0) s -= 90;
  if (canPriceFromHistory(o, now)) s += 50;
  if (withinCash(o, m, now)) s += 40;
  if (o.deliveryDays != null && o.deliveryDays >= 60) s += 20;
  // A non-each unit of issue is where newcomers lose money. Rank it lower until
  // she has some history, but never hide it.
  if (isMultiplied(o.unitOfIssue) && m.suppliedNsns.length === 0) s -= 80;
  // An order missing its unit of issue or its quantity is harder, not easier.
  if (o.unitOfIssue == null || o.quantity == null) s -= 60;
  // AN ORDER WE COULD NOT RULE ON RANKS BELOW ONE SHE MAY BID, and it is ranked
  // rather than hidden — the rule this whole file follows. Without this the
  // board opens on the 295 orders of a live 500-row page we hold no verdict
  // for, and the 55 she may actually bid sit under them.
  //
  // It is smaller than the two record bonuses on purpose. If she has WON or
  // SUPPLIED this exact stock number, her own history is the better sort key
  // and it still wins — the sort makes no permission claim either way.
  if (ourGap(o) != null) s -= 300;
  return s;
}

/**
 * The sentence on the card. First true condition wins — the same cascade as
 * whyThisAward(). Every branch is a fact she can check, not a judgement.
 */
export function whyThisOrder(
  o: MicroOrder,
  m: MicroMemberContext,
  ix: MemberIndex = memberIndex(m),
): string {
  // WE COULD NOT RULE ON THIS ONE, AND THE ROW SAYS SO BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE.
  // These rows reach the board because our gap is not her restriction, and a
  // row that says nothing about the gap is the absence of a warning doing the
  // work of a permission claim. It outranks her own record here — the record
  // lines are true and this one changes what the tap is worth.
  const gap = gapNote(o);
  if (gap != null) {
    return gap.card;
  }
  // Her own most recent answer about this part, first. The card is at the foot
  // of the board because she put it there, and the line has to say so — a card
  // that sank for a reason she cannot read is the silent version of hiding it.
  if (ix.declined.has(o.nsn)) {
    return "You said you cannot get this one.";
  }
  // She has WON this exact stock number. Her own record, in her own numbers,
  // above everything else — and stated as history, never as a prediction. We
  // do not say she will win again or that the price will hold.
  const won = ix.won.get(o.nsn);
  if (won) {
    const noun = isKnownUnit(o.unitOfIssue) ? unitNoun(o.unitOfIssue) : null;
    const when = monthYear(won.wonOn);
    return won.pricePerUnit != null && noun
      ? `You won this one in ${when} at ${usd(won.pricePerUnit)} for each ${noun}.`
      : `You won this one in ${when}.`;
  }
  if (ix.supplied.has(o.nsn)) {
    return "You have supplied this exact part before.";
  }
  if (ix.attested.has(o.nsn)) {
    return "You said you can get this one.";
  }
  // The award-history branches USED to sit here, above everything below, and
  // three things were wrong with that.
  //
  //   1. Six of nine cards on a default board read the identical sentence.
  //      A reason that is the same on six cards in a row is wallpaper, not a
  //      reason — it stopped doing the one job this line has.
  //   2. It outranked facts with a stated consequence. mp-003 is a YEAR of
  //      orders from one quote and spent its only sentence on a winner count.
  //   3. The zero case was the FIRST branch, so "no company has won this one"
  //      was presented as the reason we put the order on her board. A stock
  //      number with no award history is the hardest one on the board to
  //      source and to price, not a lead.
  //
  // Facts with a consequence she can act on now come first.
  // The rule stated four lines below applies HERE too, and did not reach this
  // branch: the card already carries the chip "A year of orders" and the caption
  // "What the first year is worth, if DLA buys the whole estimate." — so "Win
  // this one and you supply it for a year." was the one-year fact three times
  // inside ~120px, and the line that should say why THIS order ranks here was
  // spent saying its neighbour again. The unsaid fact on a repeat contract is
  // the EXPOSURE, which nothing else on the card states.
  // `=== true`, not truthiness: this SAYS something, so an unread order gets no
  // sentence rather than a wrong one. Deliberately unlike relevanceScore().
  if (o.repeats === true) {
    return "You give one price and it lasts all year.";
  }
  // No fast-award branch. `OrderCard`'s deadline chip owns the timing on a
  // fast-award order — the fact, plus the instruction on a row we ruled on — so
  // a reason that repeats it is the same sentence twice, an inch apart. One
  // fact, one owner: the foot owns timing, this line owns why the order is
  // worth her afternoon.
  // IT USED TO QUOTE THAT CHIP WORD FOR WORD, and the words moved twice under
  // it: the rail became chips on 30 August 2026, and DH-36 stood the
  // instruction down on a row we could not rule on. Nothing failed either time,
  // because a comment quoting another file is a measurement with no re-run.
  // Name the owner, never its sentence (Amendment I, instance twelve).
  // No priced-order branch either, for the same reason and against the same
  // neighbour: the caption an inch above already reads "What this order is worth
  // at the price DLA paid last time." A reason that repeats the caption is the
  // same sentence twice, ~40px apart. Deleted rather than reworded — the fact
  // she would actually want here is a competitive read of that price, and we
  // hold no figure that supports one. Abstain rather than guess.
  //
  // AND ABSTAIN HERE TOO WHEN SHE HAS TOLD US NOTHING. `matchesCategory()` is
  // vacuously true on an empty list, so on the no-category board every card fell
  // through to a claim about her business she has never made — over mop heads,
  // manifold assemblies and loop clamps. That board is not an edge she wandered
  // into: it is the route the product offers her by name ("Do you sell something
  // else? Pick nothing and Hunter shows you every order."), and CategorySheet's
  // own comment says why she takes it — none of the twelve categories is food.
  // So the one screen the persona work exists for was the one screen asserting
  // an invented fact. Settled invariant 6: abstain rather than guess.
  //
  // The empty string is the honest line, not a placeholder: the card already
  // carries its price, its unit and its closing date, and the why-slot is
  // allowed to be empty. hunterSummary() says what the board covers instead —
  // one scope sentence at the top beats the same guess repeated on every card.
  return m.categories.length === 0 ? "" : "This is the kind of thing you sell.";
}

// ── The query ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/** Orders with no stated closing date sort last rather than throwing. */
/**
 * Which order runs out of time first.
 *
 * FAST AWARD COMES FIRST, and that is the whole correction. This compared
 * `closesOn` alone, and `OrderCard` will not even PRINT `closesOn` on a
 * fast-award order — its own note says "on a fast-award order the closing date
 * is not the deadline, so it does not get to sit here pretending to be one."
 * Both statements cannot be true. If that date is not the deadline, ordering by
 * it is not ordering by when she runs out of time.
 *
 * MEASURED 2026-09-10 on one live page: 40 of 50 orders were `fastAward: true`,
 * so "Closing soon" ranked four fifths of the board by a date the board hides
 * and the code disowns. What a member sees instead is "Deliver within N days",
 * which nothing sorts by — so the control read as broken, correctly.
 *
 * DLA can buy a fast-award order AT ANY MOMENT. Nothing closes sooner than
 * that, so those rank first, and the card already says so in words: "Quote
 * today", "DLA can buy at any time." The sort and the chip now agree, which is
 * what makes the ordering legible at all.
 *
 * `fastAward` is THREE-STATE and null is not false: DLA has not run its
 * automatic evaluation, which is not the same as ruling it out. An unknown is
 * NOT treated as urgent — claiming urgency we cannot support is the harm this
 * surface refuses everywhere else — so it sorts with the dated orders on its
 * own closing date.
 *
 * A missing closing date sorts LAST within its group, unchanged: we cannot say
 * when it ends, and guessing "soon" would be the same unsupported claim.
 */
function byCloseDate(a: MicroOrder, b: MicroOrder): number {
  const aFast = a.fastAward === true;
  const bFast = b.fastAward === true;
  if (aFast !== bFast) return aFast ? -1 : 1;
  if (a.closesOn == null && b.closesOn == null) return 0;
  if (a.closesOn == null) return 1;
  if (b.closesOn == null) return -1;
  return a.closesOn.localeCompare(b.closesOn);
}

export type MicroQuery = {
  filters: MicroFilters;
  sort: MicroSort;
  q?: string;
  /** The "For you" tab: only orders `eligibleByCode()` passes reach `rows`.
   *  The server already narrows that walk; this holds when a row from an
   *  older walk is still in hand. */
  eligibleOnly?: boolean;
};

export type MicroResult = {
  /** The board, in order — what she may bid, plus what we could not rule on.
   *
   *  WIDER THAN `mayBid()` SINCE DH-34, AND DELIBERATELY. An order we hold no
   *  verdict for is not an order DLA refuses her; it is one of our own two gaps
   *  (`ourGap()`), and hiding it stated our shortcoming as DLA's restriction.
   *  Every such row carries `gapNote().card` as its sentence and the note on
   *  its own page. Membership here is NOT permission and asserts none. */
  rows: MicroOrder[];
  /** Blocked by permission — the honest tail, and its own route.
   *
   *  DLA'S REFUSAL AND NOTHING ELSE SINCE DH-34. It used to hold the orders we
   *  could not rule on as well, which put "I am not sure" on a page titled
   *  "Ones not open to you". Those are on the board now; what is left here is
   *  the tier we CAN name, which is the tier withholding actually helps her
   *  with. `notOpenToYou()` counts exactly this array. */
  restricted: MicroOrder[];
  /** EMPTY SINCE DH-34, AND THE FIELD SURVIVES ONLY BECAUSE ANOTHER FILE READS
   *  IT. Orders whose acquisition code never reached us are on the board now,
   *  so nothing is held back for an unread code and nothing can join this pool.
   *  `small-orders.restricted.tsx` still spreads it into the page it renders,
   *  and this feature does not own that file — removing the field would break
   *  its build. REPORTED for deletion together with that read and with
   *  `NO_CODE_REASON`'s grouping entries. */
  unreadable: MicroOrder[];
  /** How many we looked at in total, for Hunter's opening line. */
  considered: number;
  /** How many orders the feed handed us, BEFORE her categories, her search and
   *  the closing-date cut. Zero here means nothing arrived — a broken pipe, not
   *  a quiet day — and the two must not read the same on screen. `considered`
   *  cannot tell them apart: it is taken after scoping, so a member with one
   *  narrow category can reach zero on a perfectly healthy feed. */
  feed: number;
  /** Did a feed reach us at all? `null` in — a fetch that threw or timed out —
   *  is `false` here; an empty array is `true` with `feed === 0`. Kept as its
   *  own field rather than folded into `feed` as a nullable, because `feed` is
   *  a COUNT and a count that sometimes means "no answer" is the sentinel this
   *  file already paid for once with `amc: "0"`. */
  feedArrived: boolean;
  /** OFFERED, open, in-budget orders removed by the filter SHE set. Held back
   *  like every other exclusion, because the accounting block promises a
   *  complete account and these were in neither the list nor any bullet.
   *
   *  THE FOUR COUNTS HERE ARE TAKEN OFF THE OFFERED POOL, not off `mayBid()`.
   *  DH-34 widened the board to the orders we could not rule on, and a count
   *  narrower than the list it accounts for drops a row on every line it
   *  reaches: an unruled order over the cap would leave the board and be named
   *  on no bullet, which is the silent exclusion `D-C1` forbids. */
  filteredOut: number;
  /** Rows on the board that are worth more than the $15,000 cap. SHOWN, with
   *  the mark `overCapNote()` writes, and counted here (owner, 2026-09-16). It
   *  used to count what the cap DELETED, which is how a member read a number
   *  she could not reach. Nothing is held back for size any more. */
  overCap: number;
  /** Served orders DLA is not taking quotes on TODAY. Pending (posted, not
   *  yet open) and every terminal status — Awarded, Removed, Cancelled — which
   *  is why the line over it states the fact and forecasts no opening.
   *
   *  THE SERVER LEAVES THESE OUT of both the count and every page, so on a live
   *  board this is 0. It still counts what arrived, because a corpus the
   *  browser is handed some other way — the bundled sample, a fixture source —
   *  can hold one, and a field that lied about that would be worse than none. */
  notYetOpen: number;
  /** Served orders whose quoting status never arrived. Not the same thing as
   *  "posted, not open yet", and not promised to clear. Left out on the server
   *  too: no status is not the one status DLA takes prices on. */
  statusUnknown: number;
  /** Orders we could not classify at all — no Federal Supply Class, or one we
   *  have not mapped. SHOWN on the board since 2026-09-16 and counted here:
   *  "we could not tell what it is" must never disqualify her, in silence or
   *  otherwise. Her own category picks still filter, in `matchesCategory()`. */
  uncategorised: number;
  /** Of those, how many we could give an account of by code — plus the ones we
   *  could not classify at all, which have their OWN line. Counting an
   *  unclassifiable order in both places is how a ledger stops adding up.
   *  N in Hunter's line is `considered - readableCodes` — a SUBTRACTION of two
   *  counts from the same pool, never `(1 - ratio) * count`. Mixing a global
   *  ratio with a per-query count produced a number spoken to her that
   *  corresponded to nothing. */
  readableCodes: number;
};

/** Local date as YYYY-MM-DD, so a plain string compare against `closesOn`
 *  works with no timezone arithmetic.
 *
 *  ONE CLOCK (`D-B7`). This defaulted to `new Date()` — a third clock in a
 *  feature whose whole corpus is frozen, and the function deciding which orders
 *  are still live, so on any real day the board aged out orders the fixtures
 *  still called open. The gate that was supposed to hold this matched only a
 *  parameter literally named `now`; this one is `d`.
 *
 *  IT NOW FEEDS A DEFAULT NOTHING LIVE TAKES, and it is left standing for the
 *  same reason the `MICRO_ORDERS` default below it is: `queryBoard()` is the
 *  only seam a served board reaches `queryOrders()` through, and it always
 *  passes the day the feed arrived with. `TODAY` is the day of the bundled
 *  corpus, which is what this default is about. */
function todayIso(d: Date = TODAY): string {
  return `${d.getFullYear()}-${String(d.getMonth() + 1).padStart(2, "0")}-${String(d.getDate()).padStart(2, "0")}`;
}

/**
 * `?feed=down` — the demo switch that makes the broken-feed state reachable.
 *
 * `feedArrived: false` was designed, typed, documented and asserted, and NO
 * route in `src/` could produce it: every call site passes the static
 * `MICRO_ORDERS`, so the default `null` never fires and "I could not get
 * today's orders from DLA" could not render. A prototype with no backend is a
 * fair reason not to build error boundaries; it is not a reason to record a
 * seam as making the sentence reachable when it does not (`D-B3`).
 *
 * The override sits here rather than in the route because the route passes the
 * feed explicitly — a default cannot reach past an argument. Same shape as
 * `?sam=expired` in src/lib/state/microPurchases.ts, which exists for the same
 * reason: a designed state no product route can otherwise produce. It adds no
 * seam a developer has to inherit; deleting this function is the whole of
 * removing it.
 */
function feedIsDown(): boolean {
  if (typeof window === "undefined") return false;
  try {
    return new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get("feed") === "down";
  } catch {
    return false;
  }
}

/**
 * True when the order carries an acquisition code we can read AND a price we
 * can quote from. `queryOrders()` ranks these orders above every other order.
 */
export function isComplete(o: MicroOrder): boolean {
  return codesKnown(o) && pricePoint(o) != null;
}

export function queryOrders(
  query: MicroQuery,
  m: MicroMemberContext,
  // NULL IS A FETCH THAT FAILED. `[]` IS A FEED THAT CAME BACK EMPTY.
  // A number cannot hold both: a throw or a timeout never produces an array, so
  // `feed === 0` was asked to mean "the pipe is broken" AND "DLA posted nothing",
  // and a healthy-but-empty upstream was told the opposite of the truth. Same
  // conflation the D/H work undid one file over — "we could not READ this" is
  // not "we hold no ruling for this". Two facts, two fields, two sentences.
  all: MicroOrder[] | null = MICRO_ORDERS,
  today: string = todayIso(),
): MicroResult {
  const text = (query.q ?? "").trim().toLowerCase();
  // THE SAME DAY, IN THE FORM THE PRICE LADDER READS IT. `today` cuts the
  // closing dates and `pricing.ts` ages the awards, so one value is converted
  // once here rather than twice apart — the two halves of one board cannot
  // then be judged on different days. It is the SERVED day: `queryBoard()`
  // takes it off the feed the orders came with.
  const now = corpusNow(today);

  // Her "no" on the sourcing panel does NOT come out here, and must not. A
  // member's answer ranks; it never hides (always-on pattern 10, `D-C1`). It
  // used to be subtracted from `all` before `considered` was taken, so one tap
  // shrank the pool Hunter reports on: he said "I looked at 2" having looked at
  // five, and the empty board said "That is normal" when she had emptied it.
  // The order also lost its only route back, since the order page is reachable
  // only from the board and the answer is only re-tappable there.
  // A solicitation whose closing date has passed is not today's inventory.
  // Nothing in this feature compared closesOn to a clock, so a stale snapshot
  // kept rendering closed orders as quotable — and under "Closing soon" the
  // most expired one sorted FIRST. Sending her to a solicitation DLA has closed
  // is the same harm as sending her to one it has not opened.
  // The demo switch is read HERE, not as the default, because the route passes
  // the feed explicitly and a default cannot reach past an argument. See
  // feedIsDown().
  const feed = feedIsDown() ? null : all;
  const arrived = feed ?? [];
  const live = arrived.filter((o) => o.closesOn == null || o.closesOn >= today);

  const inScope = live.filter((o) => {
    // HER OWN PICKS ARE THE ONLY CATEGORY RULE (owner, 2026-09-16). An order we
    // could not classify is SHOWN on the board now, so it no longer needs an
    // exemption to survive a step that would have deleted it — and an exemption
    // that outlived that step would keep a row she filtered out, which is the
    // one category cut she asked for. The server applies the same picks to the
    // walk, so this is a no-op on a narrowed page and covers the moment before
    // her saved state arrives.
    if (!matchesCategory(o, m)) return false;
    if (text) {
      const hay = `${o.plainName} ${o.officialName} ${o.nsn} ${o.solicitation}`.toLowerCase();
      if (!hay.includes(text)) return false;
    }
    return true;
  });

  // THE POOL. Taken after her declines and her category scoping, and BEFORE
  // permission filtering — any later stage would exclude the very orders
  // coverage exists to count. Both numbers come from this one pool, per query.
  // They were previously taken over the whole corpus, which made Hunter speak a
  // number that was true of the catalogue and not of her board. See PRD 10.
  const considered = inScope.length;
  // An unclassifiable order counts as accounted-for here, because it is
  // disclosed on its own line below. Without this it would be subtracted twice
  // — once as "I could not read who may sell them", once as its own line — and
  // the numbers she is given to answer "am I seeing everything?" would overstate
  // what we held back.
  const readableCodes = inScope.filter((o) => isUncategorised(o) || codesKnown(o)).length;
  const uncategorised = inScope.filter(isUncategorised).length;

  // THE BOARD IS THE WHOLE POOL (owner, 2026-09-16). This used to be
  // `inScope.filter((o) => !isUncategorised(o))`, which deleted every order we
  // could not classify from the walked tabs while the chip and Hunter's
  // sentence went on counting it. Measured on the live API on 2026-09-16: one
  // member was told 120 and shown 19, and 95 of that gap was this line. An
  // order we cannot name is an order she can read for herself.
  const board = inScope;

  // WHAT THE BOARD OFFERS HER TO LOOK AT — wider than what she may bid, and
  // the two are named apart here so nothing downstream can read one as the
  // other. `mayBid()` is DLA's permission verdict and this feature does not
  // touch it. `ourGap()` is our own admission that we hold no verdict, and an
  // order carrying one belongs where she can see it: a missing acquisition
  // code is evidence we did not fetch something, never evidence she may not
  // bid. On a live 500-row page that is 295 orders of 500.
  const offered = board.filter((o) =>
    query.eligibleOnly ? eligibleByCode(o) : mayBid(o) || ourGap(o) != null,
  );
  const notYetOpen = offered.filter((o) => !acceptsQuotesNow(o) && !statusIsUnknown(o)).length;
  const statusUnknown = offered.filter(statusIsUnknown).length;

  // Restricted = we KNOW it is closed to her, and since DH-34 that is ALL it
  // is. An order we could not rule on used to land here too, so the page
  // titled "Ones not open to you" carried a group headed "I am not sure who
  // may sell these" — our gap, filed under DLA's refusal. Those are in
  // `offered` now, and the two sets stay disjoint: `ourGap()` is exactly the
  // orders `restrictionOf()` gives the `unknown` route, plus the ones it
  // refuses to speak about at all.
  const restricted = board.filter((o) => codesKnown(o) && !mayBid(o) && ourGap(o) == null);
  // Nothing is held back for an unread code any more, so nothing can join this
  // pool. See the field's own note for why it still exists.
  const unreadable: MicroOrder[] = [];

  // Permitted but not commercial: in v1 the board is commercial-only, so these
  // would have fallen out of BOTH lists silently. They belong with the ones she
  // cannot bid yet, with their own reason.

  // THE LIST SHE SCROLLS, BUILT OFF `offered` AND NOT OFF `mayBid()`. Reading
  // the verdict here would put the orders we could not rule on back behind a
  // permission test they cannot pass, which is the defect DH-34 exists to
  // close.
  //
  // IT NOW HOLDS BACK NOTHING OF ITS OWN (owner, 2026-09-16). Two cuts stood
  // here and both were invisible to the count above them:
  //
  //   · the quoting status. It is the SERVER's cut now — the corpus filter in
  //     `small-orders.mongo-feed-source.ts` admits one status, and `total`
  //     counts what it admits — so an order that cannot take a price never
  //     reaches this line and never entered the number she read.
  //   · the $15,000 cap. The row stays and carries `overCapNote()` instead. It
  //     is a fact about the order, and she can read a fact.
  //
  // Her PROFILE answers on lead time and cash are not applied here either. Both
  // rank; neither hides. See withinCash().
  let rows = offered;

  // HER OWN FILTER IS THE ONE THING THAT STILL HIDES A ROW, and it is counted.
  // The narrow sheet is deliberately different from her profile answers: she is
  // holding it open, the confirm button carries a live count, and the choice is
  // named in words. Swept over 1,008 scopings, 279 did not reconcile and every
  // one of them had a filter set.
  const beforeFilters = rows.length;
  rows = rows.filter((o) => passesFilters(o, query.filters, now));
  const filteredOut = beforeFilters - rows.length;
  // The rows she can reach that carry the mark. Counted off the LIST, so the
  // figure and the marked cards cannot disagree.
  const overCap = rows.filter((o) => overMicroCap(o, now)).length;

  // Score once per row, then sort the pairs. relevanceScore used to be called
  // inside the comparator, so a 4,000-row board made 42,336 calls, each one
  // scanning her history end to end.
  // ...and the VALUE once per row too. The hoist was applied to the identifier
  // the reviewer named and not to its sibling in the same statement, so
  // "biggest first" still called orderValue() twice per comparison — and it is
  // the more expensive of the two, because it runs pricePoint() plus a date
  // parse. Measured at 19,000 rows: 502ms to precompute and sort the pairs,
  // 24,262ms with the call left inside the comparator.
  const ix = memberIndex(m);
  const scored = rows.map((o) => ({
    o,
    s: relevanceScore(o, m, now, ix),
    v: orderValue(o, now) ?? 0,
    complete: isComplete(o),
  }));
  scored.sort((a, b) => {
    // Complete orders first, on every sort. The server walk serves them first
    // too (owner, 2026-09-15). `Array.prototype.sort` is stable, so each tier
    // keeps the order below.
    if (a.complete !== b.complete) return a.complete ? -1 : 1;
    if (query.sort === "closing") return byCloseDate(a.o, b.o);
    if (query.sort === "biggest") return b.v - a.v;
    const d = b.s - a.s;
    return d !== 0 ? d : byCloseDate(a.o, b.o);
  });
  rows = scored.map((x) => x.o);

  // Parts she told us she cannot get sink to the bottom of EVERY sort, not just
  // "Best first". A declined order arriving at the top of "Biggest first" would
  // read as Hunter ignoring her answer, which is the harm one step short of
  // hiding it.
  rows = [
    ...rows.filter((o) => !ix.declined.has(o.nsn)),
    ...rows.filter((o) => ix.declined.has(o.nsn)),
  ];

  return {
    rows,
    restricted,
    unreadable,
    considered,
    feed: arrived.length,
    feedArrived: feed != null,
    filteredOut,
    readableCodes,
    uncategorised,
    overCap,
    notYetOpen,
    statusUnknown,
  };
}

/**
 * What share of the pool we could actually read an acquisition code for.
 * The permission filter — the entire wedge — rests on `amc`/`amsc`, and both are
 * nullable. If coverage is thin, the promise "these are open to any seller" is a
 * confident wrong answer on the one thing that matters most.
 */
/**
 * Coverage of ONE result, not of the corpus.
 *
 * It used to default to the whole of MICRO_ORDERS while Hunter's line spoke
 * about the orders in front of her — a global ratio dressed up as a statement
 * about her board. Both numbers now come from the same pool.
 */
export function codeCoverage(r: Pick<MicroResult, "considered" | "readableCodes">): number {
  // An empty pool used to score 1.0 — maximum confidence — so a total ingest
  // failure could never trip the one honesty valve this surface has. There is
  // no coverage of nothing. Score it as unknown, which here is zero, so any
  // caller branching on `coverage < 0.6` gets the honest-unknown copy rather
  // than the confident one. Unknown is not generous, in this file too.
  return r.considered === 0 ? 0 : r.readableCodes / r.considered;
}

/**
 * What we held back, and why — one short line each.
 *
 * These used to be sentences appended to Hunter's line, and a board could open
 * with four of them: forty-five words, one of them thirty-three, before she
 * reached a single order. The bar is fifteen. Four true statements are still a
 * wall to someone reading English slowly on a phone.
 *
 * So the accounting is a LIST, not prose. Every count she is owed is still
 * there — that is what answers "am I seeing everything?" — and no line runs
 * past eight words. The layout carries what the sentence could not.
 */
/**
 * How many orders the "not open to you" page will show.
 *
 * ONE READER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF IT EXISTING. The board's tail
 * carried this number once before as an inline `restricted.length +
 * unreadable.length`, next to a ledger that counts the same orders under
 * `heldBack()` — two derivations of one fact, which had already drifted by the
 * time it was found, and the number was stripped rather than fixed. NO GATE
 * HOLDS "the board tail prints no second copy of the count"; this function
 * having ONE reader is what holds it. Putting it back means giving it a name
 * and one home first.
 *
 * NOT the sum of `heldBack()`. That ledger also counts orders filtered out by
 * her own category choice, and those are not on the restricted page — they are
 * not "not open to you", they are "not what you asked for". This counts exactly
 * the group that page renders.
 *
 * ONE GROUP SINCE DH-34, WHERE IT COUNTED TWO. `unreadable` is empty now, so
 * adding it would add nothing and would say the page holds a tier it does not.
 * `heldBack()`'s "need permission you do not have" line counts the SAME array,
 * which is what keeps the number she is told tied to the rows she can reach.
 */
export function notOpenToYou(r: MicroResult): number {
  return r.restricted.length;
}

/**
 * One row of the corpus census — the server's count, before any ruling.
 *
 * Mirrors `AcquisitionCodeCensus['pairs'][number]` on the backend. It is
 * declared here rather than imported because the two workspaces share no types,
 * and `api.ts` narrows the wire into this shape before anything reads it.
 */
export type CensusRow = {
  amc: string | null;
  amsc: string | null;
  hasPsc: boolean;
  dibbsStatus: string | null;
  orders: number;
};

export type CorpusLedger = {
  /** Every order on the board's corpus today. */
  total: number;
  /** Held back because DLA's codes do not let a newcomer supply the part. */
  restricted: number;
  /** We could not rule: no code reached us, or none we hold a ruling for. */
  unruled: number;
  /** The order names no product-service code, so we cannot say what it is. */
  uncategorised: number;
  /** DLA is not taking prices on it right now. */
  notTakingPrices: number;
  /** We hold the order but no status for it. */
  statusUnknown: number;
};

/**
 * The held-back ledger over the WHOLE corpus, not the page in hand.
 *
 * WHY THIS TAKES COUNTS AND NOT ORDERS. The board pages at 50, so every figure
 * derived from `MicroResult` describes the rows the browser holds — which is
 * what made Hunter say "I looked at 50 orders today" about a corpus of 13,418.
 * The server counts the corpus by the fields a ruling reads; this function does
 * the ruling, using THE SAME predicates the board and the order page use.
 *
 * A SYNTHETIC ORDER IS THE POINT, not a shortcut. `restrictionOf()`, `ourGap()`
 * and the status predicates are the only readings of these fields we have, and
 * re-implementing them over census rows would be a second owner of the
 * acquisition-code rules — the exact defect the server avoided by sending codes
 * instead of reasons. Building an order carrying just those fields runs the
 * real functions over them.
 *
 * ONE ROW LANDS IN AT MOST ONE BUCKET, in the order the ledger reads them, so
 * the parts can be summed against `total` without double counting.
 */
export function corpusLedger(rows: readonly CensusRow[], total: number): CorpusLedger {
  const out: CorpusLedger = {
    total,
    restricted: 0,
    unruled: 0,
    uncategorised: 0,
    notTakingPrices: 0,
    statusUnknown: 0,
  };
  for (const row of rows) {
    // Only the fields the predicates below read are filled. Every other field
    // takes the empty value its own three-state rule already defines, so a
    // predicate that reached for one would see "absent" and abstain — never a
    // fabricated `false`.
    // EXACTLY THE FOUR FIELDS THE PREDICATES BELOW READ, and the cast is
    // narrow for that reason: `statusIsUnknown` and `acceptsQuotesNow` read
    // `dibbsStatus`, `ourGap` and `mayBid` read the code pair, and nothing here
    // reads anything else. A fuller stand-in would invent values — a `false`
    // where the record says nothing — which is the three-state collapse this
    // codebase refuses everywhere else.
    const order = {
      amc: row.amc,
      amsc: row.amsc,
      dibbsStatus: row.dibbsStatus,
      category: row.hasPsc ? CENSUS_ANY_CATEGORY : null,
    } as unknown as MicroOrder;

    if (statusIsUnknown(order)) out.statusUnknown += row.orders;
    else if (!acceptsQuotesNow(order)) out.notTakingPrices += row.orders;
    else if (ourGap(order) != null) out.unruled += row.orders;
    else if (!mayBid(order)) out.restricted += row.orders;
    else if (!row.hasPsc) out.uncategorised += row.orders;
  }
  return out;
}

/** A category that is not null, standing for "the order names one". WHICH one
 *  the census does not carry, and no ledger line turns on which. */
const CENSUS_ANY_CATEGORY = "any" as MicroOrder["category"];

/**
 * The held-back ledger, counted over the CORPUS instead of the page.
 *
 * The wording is `heldBack()`'s, verbatim, because it is the same claim about
 * the same orders — only the pool it counts has changed. Two lines still come
 * from the page and are marked here rather than quietly mixed in:
 *
 *   · "are bigger than $15,000" is a PRICE ruling. `pricing.ts` owns it, and it
 *     withdraws a figure derived from a surplus award rather than quoting the
 *     used-goods price. The census carries no prices, and approximating them
 *     server-side would make a second owner of the one number this surface must
 *     not get wrong.
 *   · "do not match what you chose" is HER filter over the rows she was served.
 *     It is page-scoped by definition, not by omission.
 *
 * Both are therefore counted from `r`, and a reader of this function can see
 * which figures describe the day and which describe the page.
 */
export function heldBackFromCorpus(c: CorpusLedger, r: MicroResult): string[] {
  const say = (n: number, one: string, many: string) => `${n} ${n === 1 ? one : many}`;
  return [
    c.restricted > 0
      ? say(c.restricted, "needs permission you do not have", "need permission you do not have")
      : "",
    c.unruled > 0
      ? say(c.unruled, "has no clear code at source", "have no clear code at source")
      : "",
    c.uncategorised > 0
      ? say(
          c.uncategorised,
          "does not say what kind of part it is",
          "do not say what kind of part they are",
        )
      : "",
    c.notTakingPrices > 0
      ? say(c.notTakingPrices, "is not taking prices now", "are not taking prices now")
      : "",
    c.statusUnknown > 0
      ? say(c.statusUnknown, "does not say if it is open", "do not say if they are open")
      : "",
    // FROM THE PAGE, and named as such above.
    r.filteredOut > 0
      ? say(r.filteredOut, "does not match what you chose", "do not match what you chose")
      : "",
  ].filter((line) => line !== "");
}

export function heldBack(r: MicroResult): string[] {
  // COUNTED OFF THE ROWS SHE CAN NOW REACH, not off a subtraction that produced
  // the same number by a different route. `considered - readableCodes` was
  // arithmetically identical and structurally the defect: nothing tied the count
  // to any list, so the board could say "2 do not say who may sell them" over a
  // destination that held neither of them and no gate could tell.
  //
  // TWO LINES LEFT THIS BLOCK IN DH-34, AND FOUR MORE ON 2026-09-16, and the
  // rule above is why. It counts what we HELD BACK, and the heading over it
  // says so in words: "These are the ones I did not show you." A line claiming
  // we withheld a row she is looking at would be false, and four of these now
  // are: an order we could not rule on, one we could not classify and one over
  // the $15,000 cap are all ON the board, and an order DLA is not taking prices
  // on never leaves the server. Nothing is lost — `hunterSummary()` names what
  // it holds, the cap shows as a mark on the row, and the rest is one tap away.
  //
  // TWO THINGS STILL HOLD A ROW BACK: her own filter, and DLA's refusal. The
  // second is counted off `restricted`, the array `/small-orders/restricted`
  // renders and `notOpenToYou()` counts.
  const ruled = r.restricted.length;
  // "1 need permission" shipped. The verb agrees with the count here for the
  // same reason it does in hunterSummary().
  const say = (c: number, one: string, many: string) => `${c} ${c === 1 ? one : many}`;
  return [
    // HER OWN CHOICE FIRST — it is the only line here she can undo, and the
    // filter summary above already names the choice in words. Without it the
    // block promised a complete account and gave a partial one: default
    // categories with the repeats filter on showed 2 orders over six bullets
    // summing to 15, out of 20 looked at, and 3 orders were in neither place.
    r.filteredOut > 0
      ? say(r.filteredOut, "does not match what you chose", "do not match what you chose")
      : "",
    // THREE ROUTES, NOT TWO. `restrictionOf()` returns sar | none | unknown,
    // and the unknown branch exists precisely because we cannot rule. Collapsing
    // the three into one count re-asserted, one level up, the exact claim the
    // code-D decision withdrew at the order level. That tier is much smaller
    // since DFARS PGI 217.75 settled D: over `liveMapperOutput.fixture.ts` it is
    // 3 of the 30 ruled orders, against 9 before.
    ruled > 0
      ? say(ruled, "needs permission you do not have", "need permission you do not have")
      : "",
  ].filter(Boolean);
}

/**
 * What this board covers, in her words, on the screen where she asks.
 *
 * Amira is a caterer. She picks no category — none of the twelve is food — sees
 * an empty board and cannot tell whether the product is broken, whether she did
 * something wrong, or whether DLA simply does not buy what she sells here. The
 * board said nothing about its own scope.
 *
 * It asserts NO market fact. The draft said DLA buys food but not on this board,
 * and there is no sourced figure for DLA subsistence buying anywhere in the
 * binding research — a claim we cannot back, aimed at exactly the member it would
 * mislead. Abstain rather than guess. This sentence is true of the board itself,
 * which is a thing we can check.
 *
 * ON THE BOARD THAT IS ASKING THE QUESTION — settled 20 August 2026.
 *
 * That is an empty board, OR a board where she picked no category, whatever the
 * row count. The second half is the one that was missing. A member who sells
 * something we have no category for is told "Pick nothing and Hunter shows you
 * every order", and picking nothing does NOT produce an empty board — so she
 * read "What you sell: Everything" over manifold assemblies and mop heads with
 * nothing anywhere saying this board is parts only. Amira is a caterer; she is
 * exactly that member, and this sentence is written for her, so she was the one
 * member who could never read it (`D-B3`).
 *
 * It is still not on every render. Where she HAS told us what she sells the
 * scope question is answered by her own choice, and the wallpaper argument that
 * kept this off populated boards holds there — frozen decision 11 keeps the tail
 * slot on those. This takes the tail slot only where she has told us nothing.
 */
const BOARD_COVERS =
  "This board only has parts DLA buys. If you sell something else, it will not be here yet.";

/**
 * Hunter's opening line on the For you tab (owner's wording, 2026-09-15): how
 * many orders the whole board holds, and how many of them fit her profile. Both
 * numbers are the server's counts, never the rows loaded so far.
 */
export function forYouSummary(boardTotal: number, matched: number): string {
  const all = boardTotal === 1 ? "1 order" : `${boardTotal.toLocaleString("en-US")} orders`;
  const fit =
    matched === 1 ? "1 order that fits" : `${matched.toLocaleString("en-US")} orders that fit`;
  return `Out of ${all}, Hunter has read your profile and matched ${fit} your domain of work.`;
}

/** Hunter's opening line. Conditioned on how much we actually know about her. */
export function hunterSummary(
  r: MicroResult,
  m: MicroMemberContext,
  coverage = codeCoverage(r),
  /**
   * The server's count of the whole corpus, when the caller has one.
   *
   * WHY IT IS A PARAMETER AND NOT A FIELD OF `r`. `MicroResult` is what the
   * browser worked out from the rows it holds; the corpus total is what the
   * server measured. Mixing a measured figure into the derived object is how a
   * page-scoped number gets read as a corpus-wide one, which is the defect
   * this parameter exists to fix.
   *
   * IT ONLY REPLACES A STANDALONE COUNT. The clauses that PARTITION the board
   * — so many open to any seller, so many with rules, so many we could not
   * rule on — are sums over `r.rows` and must keep counting those rows, or the
   * parts stop adding up to the whole. A partition of 50 read rows printed
   * under a headline of 13,418 would be a worse lie than the one it replaced.
   */
  corpusTotal: number | null = null,
): string {
  const n = r.rows.length;
  // THE COUNT SHE READS AS "HOW MANY ARE THERE". Since DH-28 the board arrives
  // 50 at a time, so `r.rows.length` stopped meaning "today's orders" and
  // started meaning "the ones that have loaded" — and this sentence went on
  // saying "today". The server's total is the honest figure where we have it.
  const shownTotal = corpusTotal ?? n;
  /** The rows he has actually read. Every clause that PARTITIONS uses this. */
  const readOrders = n === 1 ? "1 order" : `${n.toLocaleString("en-US")} orders`;
  const orders = shownTotal === 1 ? "1 order" : `${shownTotal.toLocaleString("en-US")} orders`;
  // The verb has to agree with the count. "1 order today are open" shipped.
  const isAre = n === 1 ? "is" : "are";

  // ── The board headline reads the SAME FACT the verdict reads ─────────────
  //
  // "N orders today are open to any seller." was asserted over rows carrying a
  // set-aside — the exact sentence openVerdict() was rewritten to stop printing
  // on the order page, one surface earlier and one surface louder. On an SBA
  // total small-business set-aside it is false for anyone not small under that
  // order's size standard; on WOSB/EDWOSB/WOSB/HUBZone/8(a) it is false for
  // anyone without the SBA certification (13 CFR 128.400, FAR 19.14). Set-asides
  // are 50.5% of live DIBBS, so in production the headline was false about
  // roughly half the board every day — and OrderCard carries no set-aside mark,
  // so nothing on the board corrected it before she chose where to spend the
  // afternoon.
  //
  // The predicate is setAsideNote(), not a second reading of `o.setAside`: one
  // fact, one owner, and the surface that says "you can bid it" and the surface
  // that counts them now cannot disagree.
  //
  // WHICH set-aside it is stays on the order page, which owns that sentence.
  // The board's job is to say a condition EXISTS, in words that are true of both
  // kinds — a certificate and a size limit are different asks and one board line
  // cannot state either without being wrong about the other half.
  //
  // THE BOARD IS WIDER THAN THE VERDICT SINCE DH-34, so this line splits three
  // ways rather than two. `rows` now carries the orders we could not rule on,
  // and "N orders today are open to any seller." over 295 of them on a live
  // 500-row page would be the permission claim this whole tier exists to
  // refuse. They get their own clause and their own count, and the two clauses
  // beside them keep counting exactly what they always counted.
  const gaps = r.rows.filter((o) => ourGap(o) != null).length;
  const ruledRows = r.rows.filter((o) => ourGap(o) == null);
  const ruled = ruledRows.length;
  const ruledOrders = ruled === 1 ? "1 order" : `${ruled} orders`;
  const ruledIsAre = ruled === 1 ? "is" : "are";
  const kept = ruledRows.filter((o) => setAsideNote(o) != null).length;
  const openToAll = ruled - kept;
  const has = (c: number) => (c === 1 ? "has" : "have");
  const them = (c: number) => (c === 1 ? "it" : "them");
  // ── ONE VERB FOR "WHO IS PERMITTED", AND IT IS "SELL" ────────────────────
  //
  // This block said BOTH, inside one `.ai-surface`, on one screen: the headline
  // read "1 order today has rules about who can BID." and the held-back list
  // 40px under it read "2 do not say who may SELL them". One computed axis — who
  // DLA's acquisition codes permit to supply this order — in two vocabularies,
  // to a member reading at A2-B1 for whom two verbs are two different gates.
  //
  // "Sell" wins, and not by preference: it is the plainer word, it is already
  // the majority (`restrictionOf()`, `heldBack()` and the whole restricted page
  // use it), and the FIRST CLAUSE OF THIS VERY SENTENCE already says "open to any
  // SELLER" — so the headline did not agree with itself.
  //
  // THE RULE, so the next author does not re-split it on a coin-flip:
  //   · "who may SELL"  — third person, the question of whom DLA permits to
  //     supply an order. Every acquisition-code sentence on every surface.
  //   · "you can BID"   — second person, the question of whether she can put a
  //     price in today. `openVerdict()`'s "You can bid this one." and the empty
  //     board's "Nothing today that you can bid." stay: rows are empty for the
  //     cap and the closing date as well as for permission, and "nothing is open
  //     to you" would name permission as the reason on boards where it is not.
  //     "You can sell this one." would also claim the sale, not the permission.
  //
  // KNOWN EXCEPTION, argued not overlooked: openVerdict()'s set-aside headline
  // ("Only ${sa.who} can bid this one.") is third person and says "bid". It is a
  // different axis — who may COMPETE, by socio-economic status — and it never
  // shares a screen with a code sentence, because openVerdict() returns null on
  // every order the codes refuse. Left alone deliberately.
  //
  // CLOSED 20 Aug 2026: the order page's attribution was standardised on "sell"
  // and now reads "Who may sell this one and the plain name are my work." The
  // escalation that stood here described a defect that is no longer live, which
  // sends the next reader to redo closed work (`D-B4`).
  const ruledOpener =
    kept === 0
      ? `${ruledOrders} today ${ruledIsAre} open to any seller.`
      : openToAll === 0
        ? `${ruledOrders} today ${has(ruled)} rules about who may sell ${them(ruled)}.`
        : `${openToAll} ${openToAll === 1 ? "order" : "orders"} today ${openToAll === 1 ? "is" : "are"} open to any seller. ${kept} more ${has(kept)} rules about who may sell ${them(kept)}.`;
  // WHAT WE COULD NOT RULE ON, AS A GAP IN THE SOURCE DATA AND WITH AN OBJECT.
  // "N have no verdict" would be a fact about the order and it is not one — the
  // order is fine. It claims nothing either way about whether she may bid
  // them, which is the whole of what we know.
  // No verb agreement to carry: "does not make clear who may sell" reads the
  // same over one order and over 295, which is why this clause has no singular
  // twin.
  const opener =
    gaps === 0
      ? ruledOpener
      : ruled === 0
        ? // A PARTITION CLAIM, so it counts the rows he actually read and NOT
          // the corpus. This arm fires when every row on the board is one we
          // could not rule on; "I could not rule on 13,418 orders today" would
          // claim he had read the whole corpus and failed on all of it.
          `The source data does not make clear who may sell ${readOrders} today.`
        : `${ruledOpener} The source data does not make clear who may sell ${gaps} more.`;

  // Orders we could not classify are excluded and SAID OUT LOUD. A separate
  // sentence from the permission tier below, because it is a different reason:
  // there we could not read who may sell it, here we could not tell what the
  // thing is. Conflating the two would be a tidier lie.
  // NOTHING ARRIVED. This has to be said before anything below, and it used to
  // be unreachable: the `n === 0` return sat above the coverage test, so a total
  // ingest failure and a quiet Tuesday rendered the identical sentence and the
  // honesty valve codeCoverage() was fixed to arm could never fire.
  //
  // The test is `feed`, not `considered`, deliberately: `considered` is taken
  // after her categories and the closing-date cut, so a member with one narrow
  // category hits zero on a healthy feed — and telling her the pipe is broken
  // there would be a new false statement. `feed` is what the ingest handed us.
  // No promise about when it comes back, because we do not know.
  if (!r.feedArrived) {
    return "Today's orders from DLA did not load. Check again later.";
  }
  // The feed answered, and the answer was nothing. Not the same fact, so not the
  // same sentence: telling her the pipe is broken here would be a new false
  // statement, and "Nothing today that you can bid" below would be a true
  // statement about the wrong thing — it reads as "none of them suit you" when
  // there were none at all.
  //
  // It also does not claim the pipe is HEALTHY. An empty array is genuinely
  // ambiguous — a silent 500 and a quiet Tuesday both produce one — so the
  // sentence states what we can see and attributes no cause. `null` is the only
  // unambiguous failure, and it gets the only sentence that names one. No
  // promise about tomorrow, because we hold none.
  if (r.feed === 0) {
    return "There are no orders today. Check again later.";
  }

  // Read the codes BEFORE the count. A board of nothing built out of orders we
  // could not read is not a quiet day either, and the bullets below already say
  // so in the same words — the fact about the order, no confession, no blame.
  if (coverage < 0.6) {
    // "in what you sell" is a claim about her business, so it may only be made
    // where she made it. On the no-category board `matchesCategory()` is
    // vacuously true and this scoped the pool to a trade she never named — the
    // same vacuous-true defect as whyThisOrder()'s fallback, on the sentence
    // above it. Swept together (`D-F5`).
    const scoped =
      m.categories.length > 0 ? `${orders} today in what you sell.` : `${orders} today.`;
    return n === 0
      ? `Nothing today that you can bid. Many of these do not say who may sell them. ${BOARD_COVERS}`
      : `${scoped} For many of these, the source data does not say who may sell them. Read the order paper before you spend time on one.`;
  }

  if (n === 0) {
    // Say what is true: nothing she can bid TODAY, and everything we held back —
    // and then answer the question an empty board actually raises for her.
    return `Nothing today that you can bid. ${BOARD_COVERS}`;
  }
  // SHE PICKED NOTHING, SO THE SCOPE QUESTION IS THE ONE BEING ASKED — and it
  // is asked ABOVE the coverage tests, because on the no-category board
  // coverage is 0.909 and the `< 0.95` return below took the sentence away
  // before it could ever ride. See BOARD_COVERS.
  if (m.categories.length === 0) {
    return `${opener} ${BOARD_COVERS}`;
  }
  if (coverage < 0.95) {
    // Was "look open to any seller". Every row here passed the permission rule
    // on codes we could read, and the order page states the same fact flat
    // ("DLA lets anyone quote this part") — so the board was the less certain
    // of the two about one fact. The orders we could NOT read are disclosed on
    // their own line; they are not a reason to hedge about these.
    return opener;
  }
  // The scope sentence has already taken the tail slot on the no-category
  // board, above. Both sentences are context beyond the count and only one can
  // ride at 390px — this file already paid for a board that opened with
  // forty-five words of accounting — so this is the tail slot wherever she HAS
  // told us what she sells, which is every board that reaches here.
  const knowsHer = m.suppliedNsns.length > 0 || m.attestedNsns.length > 2;
  if (!knowsHer) {
    // Frozen decision 11: the money is small and the product says so. Said once,
    // early, before she spends an afternoon on a $200 order and feels misled.
    return `${opener} The money on these is small. What you really get is a finished job on your record.`;
  }
  // `considered` is the size of the pool we actually looked at. Never dress it up
  // as the whole DLA board — that would be a number we did not earn.
  return opener;
}

export { usd };
